/** Slot3 MT-garbage fixes — batches 1–2 */
export const BLOCKS = {
  "siman_202/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|8|_": `More than 181 — Tur and Shach, and as written in Chagigah there from the sugya that ascends [to the heavens].`,
  "siman_202/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|9|_": `Handles — mishnah 5 chapter 10, and as Tosafot.`,

  "siman_203/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|2|_": `His ledger — meaning they open from Heaven a ledger of his deeds and examine them.`,
  "siman_203/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|3|א": `And this applies [when he vowed in general]; but if he vowed for charity or for consecration, etc. — and see above siman 258.`,
  "siman_203/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|3|ב": `And likewise if he swore — meaning that in an oath it is written "he shall not go unpunished," and therefore they are stringent regarding an oath, for it has no release, as in Tur and Beit Yosef below siman 230; and see there.`,
  "siman_203/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|4|_": `Say without vow — Rashi's intent that he should say explicitly "without vow"; but it does not mean he should stop casually and not say he does so because of a vow, for even if he stops casually it is a vow. Perishah. And see above siman 257 seif 3.`,
  "siman_203/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|6|א": `It is permitted for him to vow, etc. However, even in casual speech, if he says "I will repeat" there is upon him the obligation of a vow, as below siman 213 seif 2; and so Rabbeinu Yerucham, part 3, and Semag daf 71 end of 73, and other poskim.`,
  "siman_203/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|6|ב": `It is a mitzvah to swear, etc. — although an oath does not take effect on a matter of mitzvah, as below siman 239, nevertheless it is permitted for a person to urge himself. Shach.`,

  "siman_203/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|3|_": `The tanna of Rav Dimi, brother of Rav Safra, from the verse mentioned — Ketubot daf 77.`,

  "siman_203/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|_": `He delayed — Yerushalmi chapter 1, and Rosh brought it in chapter 1.`,
  "siman_203/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3|א": `And this applies — Nedarim 49a, and Ran s.v. terumah, etc.`,

  "siman_204/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|2|_": `Unspecified — Taz and Shach wrote that the Rav's words refer only if he attached to a thing, such as if he said "this loaf is upon me like the sacred table," or even "like a table" unspecified is also forbidden like a lamb and birds; but one who vows "on the table" without attaching to anything, but only says in oath language "I vow" or "I swear on the table," whether sacred or unspecified — it is not an oath at all; and Rama's words here are like an error from before the ruler — end of his words.`,

  "siman_204/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|א": `The main point — Nedarim 12a, 14a; from where do we know, etc.; Shevuot 20b: Rava said, answer, etc.; and Ra'avan beginning of Nedarim s.v. kol kinuyei, etc.`,
  "siman_204/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ב": `Or, etc. — there 1a: issurei de'issur, etc.`,
  "siman_204/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ג": `Or, etc.; and likewise if such [language] is forbidden — meaning even if he did not mention eating meat and drinking wine. Rosh chapter 2 of Nedarim, and Rashi in Shevuot there s.v. taritz, etc.; and how is "attached," etc.; and Tosafot there s.v. ella, etc.; and see in Shulchan Aruch siman 239 seif 9. (Likut) And likewise if, etc. — not like Rashi; and see siman 239 seif 9, the Rif's approach (end).`,
  "siman_204/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|א": `If he vowed, etc. — as explained in Shulchan Aruch, or as one of all, etc.`,
  "siman_204/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ב": `But if he called, etc. — Taz and Shach, and so in Shulchan Aruch siman 237 seif 6; and his proof from what is written 14b: one who vows by the Torah said nothing, for machataa araa; and layman's language, etc.; and as written above (א).`,
  "siman_204/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3|_": `(Likut) I heard, etc., and I like you, etc. — Rambam's language; but RAN wrote in chapter 3 of Shevuot that if he said "like you" he does not need twenty-four, etc.; and as written in siman 239, see there; and likewise at the beginning of Nazir we say that if he said "I am like this one" he is a nazir, and so is the custom (end).`,
  "siman_204/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4|_": `A man, etc. — Ran Nedarim chapter 5, 2, s.v. amar Abaye, etc.; and from this, etc. (and so in the Gemara there 47a); and Hagahot Ashiri in Ketubot chapter 5, 49a, s.v. veheikha. And even Rashba, etc., that regarding himself remains Ran's precision [Nedarim 30b from those that are born, etc.].`,

  "siman_205/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|א": `One who says, etc. — Rambam's wording.`,
  "siman_205/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ג": `Some say, etc. — Tosafot Nedarim 19, 6a, s.v. veha'idna, etc.; and one may distinguish; and Ra'avan s.v. ha, etc., ha, etc.`,
  "siman_205/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|א": `One who says, etc. — as written in 61, and above siman 208 seif 1; and there "if the majority," etc.; and likewise now the reverse. Shach. And as in mishnah 18:2: R. Yossi, etc.; in Galilee it is permitted, etc.`,

  // batch 2 — simanim 264, 283, 293
  "siman_264/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|5|_": `The statement of Rav Chisda — Chullin daf 16b.`,
  "siman_264/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ב": `A woman, etc. — Rav and R. Yehuda: the halachah follows R. Yehuda, as written in the first chapter of Yevamot (4).`,
  "siman_264/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|6|_": `That they must return — see responsum Sha'agat Aryeh siman 54, who expanded to prove like the poskim that one need not return and let blood of the covenant flow; see there.`,
  "siman_264/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 264, proof 1) His brothers died from circumcision — it implies that an apostate regarding foreskin, even if not to provoke, is unfit to circumcise. Peri Chadash in his book part 5 (section 3) challenged this; his view is that anyone who is not [circumcising] to provoke is fit to circumcise, as he is called "bar zevichah" above (siman 2 seif 2). See responsum Beit Yaakov (siman 104) and in Even HaEzer (Orach Chayim siman 289).`,
  "siman_264/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|2|_": `(There in the gloss) An apostate regarding foreskin — his law is like a gentile. It is astounding to me, for from Tosafot's words, from what they asked why he did not say that an apostate regarding foreskin is not included in keeping the covenant, as we say in Gittin that an apostate is not fit for ritual slaughter; and according to the one who says "the circumcised shall circumcise," it is resolved that an apostate regarding foreskin is like one who is circumcised and is fit according to R. Yehuda; and since we hold that a woman is fit to circumcise, as Rif, Rambam, Rosh, and Tur wrote, because we hold that when no man is present she is fit — and in any case if she circumcised, one need not let blood of the covenant flow — the same applies to an apostate regarding foreskin. To say Tosafot deals with not for provocation and Rama with not for provocation, in which case he is not like one circumcised — this is forced, for Tosafot too must deal with not for provocation; when for provocation he is called fit for slaughter as he is called fit for slaughter. To say Tosafot is when not for provocation and not to provoke but he is not careful to circumcise, and Rama when to provoke — though Rama's wording is difficult to explain — nevertheless from Shach's wording (s.k. 1) who wrote "but if he intends to break the covenant" it does not imply that only when intending to break the covenant is he unfit; but in my view one cannot say so, for if Rama deals with provoking, what is the relevance of "apostate regarding foreskin even in one matter" — behold to provoke he is a heretic and apostate to the entire Torah, as in Shulchan Aruch above (siman 2 seif 4); and especially that even regarding apostate to entire Torah it appears Tosafot's view is to permit, for according to their answer "you bound them" is in a place of binding (and one must say that for provocation he is called one who upholds binding and slaughter). Requires further study.`,
  "siman_264/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|5|_": `(Shach s.k. 9) And it is not precise. See Tevuot Shor on Yevamot (daf 137a).`,
  "siman_264/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|א": `His brothers died, etc. — but he intends to break the covenant, not [to circumcise properly].`,
  "siman_264/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|ד": `His law is like a gentile — meaning regarding circumcision his law is like a gentile; but for other matters an apostate regarding foreskin is not like a gentile, for an apostate in one matter is not an apostate to the entire Torah, as above simanim 90 and 119.`,
  "siman_264/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|ו": `However, if strands remained, etc. — meaning: if strands remained, etc., he must finish according to its law, i.e., remove the strands or perform periah; but where nothing remained, one must let blood of the covenant of circumcision, for within eight days even if done by a Jew it is not considered circumcision at all. But he does not wish to say that if no strands remained one need not even let blood flow, for within eight days it is not called circumcision; and also above siman 262 s.k. 2 I raised that one must let blood flow if circumcised within eight days. And know that also regarding one circumcised by a gentile these words apply here, and by a gentile even according to the Rav one must let blood flow — but certainly he does not come to say that one need not let blood flow; requires study.`,
  "siman_264/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|ז": `And acquisition does not help here — for it is merely an informal agreement; this is Rosh's responsum. It is possible Rosh spoke only that the court cannot compel him, but nevertheless he agrees to Rambam that there is [prohibition] because of "the remnant of Israel shall not do iniquity nor speak lies," and one may call him wicked; and even though he wrote there "and even though Maharam wrote in a responsum to follow the custom, it does not appear to me," etc. — meaning it is not acquisition through custom as implied in Mordechai above in the name of Maharam who holds that through custom there is acquisition and he cannot retract, similar to a sealed barrel that is acquired; but he agrees to Maharam that it is forbidden to retract initially, and so his wording implies in the responsum — end of his words.`,
  "siman_264/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|6|ב": `Since he was circumcised once properly — meaning: and one should say according to above seif 5 that the majority of the corona must be revealed, because there he had not yet been circumcised according to its law; but here, since he was already circumcised according to its law by Torah, even if he did not appear circumcised at all, even when he becomes an adult he would not be called uncircumcised except rabbinically, since he appears circumcised only at the head of the corona and below a bit appears circumcised, we call him circumcised; and so wrote Tur siman 264, and Bach wrote. However, where he was born circumcised but not completely, such that skin covers the corona in most of its height or most of its circumference — even if he already let blood of the covenant on the eighth day of his birth — since afterward even when he becomes an adult he appears only partially circumcised, still covering most of the height of the corona or most of its circumference, he must circumcise him and cut all the flesh from here and hence until he appears fully circumcised at the time of examination; and certainly one need not cut anything except when they circumcised him initially according to its law; but one who was not circumcised at all, his law is like a baby who was circumcised and has strands covering most of the height of the corona that impede circumcision — for thus it seems to me regarding one born thus, as if the foreskin was cut at the time of circumcision and strands cover most of its height — both this and that, his skin was never cut in any way according to its law. And in Beit Yosef in the name of a Spanish sage it appears he was more stringent, that he wrote that the examination at becoming an adult is stated only for children who are heavy and dangling who were circumcised initially according to its law; but for all others the corona must be revealed even not at the time of examination, etc.; behold he was stringent that for one not circumcised initially according to its law one must cut flesh until the corona is revealed even not at examination; and so I ruled in practice — end. And to me the words of this sage require study, for we learn in Yerushalmi chapter R. Eliezer from the baraita: these are the strands that impede circumcision — R. Avina in the name of R. Yirmiyah said: when it covers most of the height of the corona, etc.; R. Tavi in the name of R. Shmuel said: we examine him at the time he becomes an adult — end; thus even without circumcision according to its law, Shmuel too said we examine at becoming an adult; requires study.`,
  "siman_264/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|ב": `And some say a woman, etc. — in the Gemara they disagree in the reason a gentile may not circumcise: Rav said because of "and you shall keep My covenant"; R. Yochanan said because it is written "the circumcised shall circumcise" — he shall circumcise, the circumcised one shall circumcise. What is between them? Abaye said: a woman — for Rav there is none, for a woman is not subject to circumcision; and for R. Yochanan there is, for a woman is like one who is circumcised and is fit; and the halachah was decided like Rav.`,
  "siman_264/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|ד": `If strands remained, etc. — this is from Rashba's responsum in Beit Yosef, and it implies that if no strands remained a Jew need do nothing else on the eighth day — and this is like the view the Mechaber ruled: if a gentile circumcised for a Jew, one need not return and circumcise again, and even need not let blood flow; and this is the main novelty of Rashba, for otherwise why would Rashba write this matter, for if strands remained it is obvious; rather he comes to precision that when none remained nothing is needed. And if so, since Rama ruled like the first view that they must return and let blood, etc., here too one must let blood of the covenant on the eighth day even if no strands remained; and Rama should not have written "if strands remained," etc.`,

  "siman_283/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|2|_": `A scroll — the view of Rif and other poskim is that it is permitted because of "a time to act for the Lord, they have voided Your Torah," for not everyone knows by heart and not everyone in the world has chumashim in the synagogue, and they would come to nullification of Shema and prayer; and Bach wrote that such is the widespread custom in all lands — end. And the reason one does not write the Torah as a separate scroll is likewise one does not write from the Torah a scroll for a child even for learning, for specifically a chumash by itself is permitted, but not scroll by scroll — end of Shach. And Taz wrote that what Bach wrote that the custom is to permit is in order to learn from it because of "a time to act," etc.; but those who write verses on the wall for some good sign do not act properly, for the Torah was given sealed.`,
  "siman_283/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3|_": `Not so, and one may not, etc. — Gittin, a question that was resolved, and like the first tanna. Rambam and Rosh and Shulchan Aruch. And Rif permits, as written regarding the haftarah scroll.`,
  "siman_283/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|2|_": `From the produce of the five species — see responsum Beit Ephraim Yoreh De'ah siman 68, who wrote that grain that did not reach a third [ripening] has here [prohibition] of chadash as it is obligated in challah; see there.`,
  "siman_283/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|2|_": `(Shach s.k. 6) That it is forbidden to write verses from the Torah. See responsum Mahari"t (part 2, Orach Chayim siman 3).`,
  "siman_283/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|2|א": `One may not write the Torah scroll by itself, etc. — the view of Rif, Mordechai, Agudah, and other poskim is that it is permitted because of "a time to act for the Lord, they have voided Your Torah," for not everyone knows by heart and not everyone in the world has chumashim in the synagogue, and they would come to nullification of Shema and prayer; and so Bach, and he wrote that such is the widespread custom in all lands.`,
  "siman_283/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|2|ב": `And one may not write, etc. — meaning: for the same reason one may not write the Torah as a separate scroll, one may not write from the Torah a scroll for a child even for learning, for specifically a chumash by itself is permitted, but not scroll by scroll.`,
  "siman_283/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|4|_": `It is forbidden to embroider verses on a tallit — for the reason above that it is forbidden to write even one verse; and further, because the tallit, even though it has tzitzit, is merely a utensil of mitzvah and not of inherent sanctity; therefore one may enter with a tzitzit tallit into the privy and the bathhouse and use it and cover nakedness with it — how can one bring sacred writings to a place of filth and contempt? So wrote Taz; and for this reason they permitted writing verses on the cloths of a Torah scroll, because they are utensils of sanctity and one may not treat them lightly — end of his words. And even though Rabbeinu Yerucham there wrote another reason for the prohibition, namely that it is forbidden to write verses from the Torah — it has already been explained that the custom spread to be lenient in this; and see Rabbeinu Yerucham there, who brought Rambam's responsum that expanded on this.`,

  "siman_293/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|_": `From the produce of the five species — mishnah in the first chapter of Challah and chapter 6 of Menachot (71a).`,
  "siman_293/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|_": `The prohibition, etc. — so wrote all the early compilations, and not like Semak; and Rosh already wondered at him and wrote that the anonymous mishnah at the end of Orlah is like R. Eliezer. And even though apparently the anonymous mishnah in chapter 49 of Menachot is like the first tanna, as written chapter 4, 1, like one who does not hold, etc., and he holds, etc. — the tanna of the mishnah does not hold so but explains his reason; but the tanna of the mishnah holds "when you come" depends on the land, and therefore Rambam ruled like the mishnah there, and so in Choshen Mishpat, see there. But all this is unnecessary, for we hold like the amoraim there 68b that all hold it applies outside the Land, and even R. Papa and R. Huna hold that nevertheless rabbinically one is obligated; and even in this we do not hold like him, for the sugya of halachah in all places is like the rabbis of R. Eliezer, and Ravina in the last [sugya] holds so and we hold like him, as written in 61. And the words of Behag here are a great error and need not be answered; and also what he wrote in the name of Bach — Bach holds it does not apply to gentiles' [grain], and all the later authorities have struck him on his head, and an error escaped from his hand; and it would have been better for Behag here to remain silent. And Maharshal (siman 489) wished to justify Semak's words, but his words are very forced, and he wrote himself that he wished to justify the custom; but a person of [refined] soul, etc. — but his words are not worthwhile even to justify the custom. And Taz wrote that in time of pressure it is worthwhile, etc.; but in the Gemara in Niddah there they said that if halachah was decided, tell him "time of pressure" — and it was already decided here in Menachot there (and so in Nachalat Kehuna, see there, and in Or Zaru'a, Laws of Orlah and Chadash siman 328). Rather, the leniency spread because they would sow before Pesach, as written in 63 in the gloss; and leave Israel — better, etc. (as written in Shabbat 148b and chapter 4 of Yevamot). (Likut) See in the gloss of Behag what he wrote as proof from Yerushalmi beginning Challah — but he erred, and it is in chapter 2, and thus Yerushalmi there [and Tosafot brought it in Rosh Hashanah 13a s.v. de'akrivu, etc.] — requires study.`,
  "siman_293/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_": `To eat chadash — Avodat HaTaharah of Mahari"t, and what he wrote "one who practices," etc.; see in responsum Peri Megadim. And see on this in responsum Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 16, who disagreed with Or Zaru'a and Peri Megadim on this. And see in responsum Geonei Batra'i, responsum Bach Chadashot, Shav Yaakov, responsum Or Zaru'a Pnei Me'orot part 1 siman 107, part 3 siman 34 [and responsum part 8 siman 500], and in Sefer Pe'er Yitzchak klal 101 to Kiddushin, responsum Knesset Yechezkel, responsum Ma'amar Mordechai — several responsa on the matter, Yad Eliyahu siman 24, Mayim Rabim siman 44, Sefer HaMakneh beginning of Kiddushin, and Sefer Shem Chadash from the Gaon Maharal of Piltz, responsum Beit Ephraim Yoreh De'ah siman 65, Tiferet Tzvi part Orach Chayim siman 26, part Yoreh De'ah siman 64, Teshuvat Chen siman 25 and 26, responsum Kiryat Chana siman 46, and Sefer Nachalat Azriel in Chullin daf 120 [and responsum Mishkenot Yaakov siman 64 and 68 at length].`,
  "siman_293/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|2|_": `From the produce of the five species — see responsum Beit Ephraim Yoreh De'ah siman 68, who wrote that grain that did not reach a third has here prohibition of chadash as it is obligated in challah; see there.`,
  "siman_293/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|3|ה": `For it is better that they be inadvertent, etc. — but if most of the country's drinking is wine and barley is not needed except occasionally for food — this is forced; the community is not in a position to separate from chadash prohibition where there is only one doubt, whether from last year or this year; it is a mitzvah to protest to the masses to teach them the prohibition, for a doubt here is not [resolved as] last year's surplus. And if you say this year's grain — the joy of this harvest is from the minority sown before the omer, distinguished from the omer, for since it is a minority, we do not make from it a doubt against the majority, as explicitly in Tosafot in the sugya opening Ketubot — end; and in Sefer Choshen Mishpat end of tractate Terumot he expanded to find permission for barley beer made from chadash because the liquid extracted from them is permitted; and the matter requires settlement, for from Rosh's responsum beginning of klal 2 and from Terumat HaDeshen cited and from other poskim it implies it is forbidden.`,
  "siman_293/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|3|ו": `Better that they be inadvertent, etc. — see in Orach Chayim siman 248 seif 2 in the gloss.`,
  "siman_293/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|_": `It is forbidden to eat chadash, etc. — it seems to me that dough kneaded with leaven of chadash, the entire dough is forbidden and nullification in sixty does not help here for two reasons. First, we find in the Gemara chapter "One may not establish" in the name of Rabbeinu Tam in his dispute with R. Chanina regarding yeast of yayin nesech: "and if placed in dough, the entire dough is forbidden, for everything done for taste, even in a thousand it is not nullified," etc.; and so in Shulchan Aruch siman 123 seif 15 above. Second, because it is chadash, a thing that has permitted forms, and even in a thousand it is not nullified, as Rosh wrote in responsum beginning of klal 2 regarding beer made from chadash barley, and he brings proof from Yerushalmi that said so explicitly. I wrote this because I saw some are careful about chadash but in this matter they are not careful, and it is an error in their hands.`,
  "siman_293/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2|_": `Whether from gentiles' [grain] — so wrote the poskim in explanation, and Mahari Chagiz wrote that there is clear proof that gentiles' [grain] is not subject to chadash, from the first chapter of Rosh Hashanah (daf 13): they asked Rav Kahana: the omer that Israel offered when they entered the Land — from where did they offer it? You would say it entered by gentile hands — "your harvest" the Merciful One said, not gentile harvest. From where that they offered? Perhaps they did not offer? It cannot enter your mind, for it is written "and they ate from the produce of the land on the morrow after Pesach" — on the morrow after Pesach they ate; initially they did not eat, for they offered the omer; from where did they offer? He said: anything that did not bring a third by gentile hands. Now if you say chadash applies even to gentiles' [grain], he would not need to prove "initially they did not eat" — in any case he should have said: if they did not offer, how could they eat on the morrow after Pesach, behold it is forbidden because of chadash? Rather, necessarily chadash does not apply to gentiles' [grain]; and therefore he inferred "initially they did not eat" — for if it entered by gentile hands and they did not offer the omer initially, why did they not eat? Behold with gentiles' [grain] there is no chadash prohibition; or they offered the omer from Israel's [grain], and therefore they did not eat initially from Israel's [grain]; from where did they offer? — end. And I say: his soul rests in goodness, but he was not precise in these matters, for what he asked — why did the Gemara not ask from where they ate, behold forbidden because of chadash — there is not even the beginning of a question, for it is possible they ate from old grain from last year; it is possible it became clear to them that this is old grain, and even in doubt Rosh wrote in responsum in the name of Avi HaEzri that it is permitted, for there is a double doubt; and Tur too wrote — end; therefore the questioner needed to prove from "initially they did not eat" that there was chadash prohibition there, and on this he asks from where was it permitted, and on Mahari Chagiz's explanation that he concludes they offered the omer from Israel's [grain], etc., there is to wonder: if so, why did he ask from where Israel took grain for the omer — he should have asked more: from where did Israel take grain to eat, since now we hold that the verse "and they ate from the produce of the land" refers to Israel's [grain]? Rather it is clear that even gentiles' [grain] is forbidden, as all the poskim wrote; and from this is clear proof of prohibition, for at that time all the grain was gentiles' according to the questioner's view, and nevertheless there was chadash prohibition — only that he was asked from where they took the omer.`,
  "siman_293/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|3|ב": `But one may not rule for others, etc. — it is not worthwhile to justify on account that we see sages of Israel, most of them, are not careful about chadash in any year, and it is not applicable to say of them "better they be inadvertent," etc.; and Mahari Chagiz already toiled on this matter to find permission from Yerushalmi, from which R. Yitzchak author of Tosafot proved there is prohibition even in gentiles' [grain]; and afterward all the great ones followed and said he has another explanation of Yerushalmi not like R. Yitzchak's words. We have no business with this; even if we had a correct explanation of Yerushalmi, we may not raise our head against all the poskim. Nevertheless it appears according to my humble view to teach adequate merit on the permission in this, for this prohibition of chadash outside the Land is a dispute of tannaim in the first chapter of Kiddushin (38a): the first tanna of the mishnah holds chadash does not apply outside the Land, as written therein "in all your dwellings," meaning only after inheritance and settlement; and R. Eliezer holds chadash applies everywhere — "your dwellings" wherever you dwell implies; and R. Yishmael and R. Akiva also disagree, R. Yishmael like the first tanna and R. Akiva like R. Eliezer. And in Rosh's responsum he wrote R. Yishmael and R. Akiva hold like R. Eliezer — and this is an error, for we explicitly say in the Gemara: Abaye said, who is the tanna who disagrees with R. Eliezer? R. Yishmael; and one must say R. Akiva holds like R. Eliezer. And it is astounding on Beit Yosef who copied this responsum and did not notice the error that contradicts the entire world — requires study.`,

  // batch 3 — simanim 246, 247
  "siman_246/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|8|_": `Better — Shach wrote that in the Gemara we say: and R. Meir, how did he learn Torah from another, whereas Rav Nachman said, what is written "the lips of a priest," etc.; and the Gemara distinguishes between an adult and a minor; and therefore Rambam and his supporters should also distinguish in this matter. And one may say he holds as Tosafot wrote there: in Moed Katan regarding one who was intoxicated, etc., one may say they are minors who study before him and we are concerned lest they lead him astray — if so, we see that even in the days of the Gemara there were minors, and all the more so nowadays when all are considered minors, as written "if the early ones were like angels," etc.; and therefore nowadays there is no distinction. And the Gaon Mahari Chagiz answered that he holds: since the Gemara says R. Meir found a verse and expounded from it, it implies R. Meir held thus, and we do not hold like him; and similarly poskim wrote regarding R. Meir "he does not eat bishul Yisrael," etc.; see there — end.`,
  "siman_246/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|24|_": `Shabbat daf 46, beginning of 72.`,
  "siman_246/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ז": `One who cannot, etc. — Bereishit Rabbah 99: Zevulun shall dwell at the shore of the seas — behold Zevulun preceded Issachar; why so? Because Zevulun engaged in commerce and Issachar in Torah, and Zevulun came and fed him; therefore Scripture preceded him: "It is a tree of life to those who grasp it," etc.; and likewise Moshe says, "Rejoice, Zevulun, in your going out" — because Issachar is yours, for you support him to dwell in them, etc.; and in Vayikra Rabbah 25: "Tree of life," etc. — if it had said "tree of life to those who toil in it," etc.; "for in the shadow of wisdom," etc.; in the future the Holy One will make shade and a canopy for doers of mitzvot near Torah scholars in the World to Come. And we have three readings: one "for in the shadow," etc.; two "happy is the man who does this," etc.; three "this is the tree of life," etc.; Shimon brother of Azaryah, etc., said in his name: was not Shimon greater than Azaryah? And similarly for Zevulun he said "Rejoice," etc.; and in chapter 4 of Pesachim (53b).`,
  "siman_246/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ח": `And a person may, etc.; but, etc. — Sotah 21a.`,
  "siman_246/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|10|ב": `Rather he repeats, etc. — Eruvin 54b, Ra'avad, etc.`,
  "siman_246/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|4|_": `Other wisdoms — see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 210 that one should distance learning Kabbalah, for our knowledge is short and "the Merciful One exempts the compelled"; see there at length. And see on this responsum Maharanach siman 8, responsum Shemesh Tzedakah Yoreh De'ah siman 61 in the middle of the responsum, responsum Teshuvah MeAhavah part 1, and see in Noda B'Yehudah Yoreh De'ah siman 93.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|א": `And in time of pressure, etc. — and so in Semag positive commandment 12, concluding "and this thing is forbidden to say before a gentile"; however in Hagahot Maimoni he does not bring this conclusion, and afterward the Rav continues. It appears the reason we learn in Menachot (99b): R. Yochanan said in the name of R. Shimon ben Yochai: even if a person read only Shema morning and evening, he fulfills "it shall not depart"; and this thing is forbidden to say before a gentile; and Rava said it is a mitzvah to say it before a gentile. Rashi explained: he holds that because of Shema one receives such great reward — if he were occupied all day, how much more so his reward is great, and he accustoms his sons to Torah. Others explain: those rabbis could exempt themselves with Shema and sit all day and study — from this, great reward; end. If so, since Rava said it is a mitzvah to say it, we hold like him; and all the more so according to the version of Yalkut at the beginning of Yehoshua, where we learn: Abaye cursed one who says it before a gentile, and Rava said it is a mitzvah — if so we hold like Rava against Abaye; also in Midrash Tehillim it is anonymous in the name of R. Yehoshua ben Levi: even if one read only Shema morning and evening he fulfills, and "in his Torah he meditates day and night," and Yalkut brings this at the beginning of Tehillim; see there.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|ב": `And he shall share wages with him — meaning the wage of Torah and the wage of what this one earns shall be between them together.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|ג": `But, etc. — he cannot sell his share, etc.; so it should read.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|4|א": `And some say that in the Gemara, etc. — Derishah wrote: some householders practice learning Gemara every day and not other poskim; they bring proof from what we say at the end of Niddah: Tanna deVei Eliyahu — whoever repeats halachot every day is assured he is a child of the World to Come. But to my mind one must learn books of poskim, laws of the Torah, like Rif, Mordechai, Rosh and the like, for this is the root and essence of our Torah; and they do not leave at all to study Gemara alone, for that which Tanna deVei Eliyahu, etc. — Rashi already wrote there "who repeats halachot" means halachot of verses; and what Rabbenu Tam wrote here "Gemara" means Bavli mixed, etc., refers to what was written before this — nine hours a day of study; since he has much free time he should learn Gemara; but those householders who learn only three or four hours should not learn Gemara alone, as above — end.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|9|א": `And some say, etc. — and Ran further distinguishes: for soft teachings the students stand; for requests they sit; and in Ein Yaakov, chapter "One who reads standing," he wrote that when they repeated they all stood, for they did not need to analyze; but in reasoning where they need to analyze, the rabbi sat and others stood; sometimes they all sat — when they needed to analyze and toil in halachah — end.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|9|ב": `And he did not write them, etc. — and see in Orach Chayim siman 145, who wrote laws of the translator regarding Torah reading.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|13|_": `And some say, etc. — thus the Rav brought from Tosafot in Bekhorot (36a); and it requires study, for Tosafot did not write there except regarding the act of R. Tzadok, that R. Gamliel told the questioner to stand and ask, etc.; and Tosafot wrote: the questioner stood and asked — Yerushalmi in Nedarim, chapter Na'arah, derives from "and the two men shall stand" with a vav on "the questioner" — laws and aggadot require standing — end; and this is certainly the law before the destruction of the Temple, for R. Gamliel before the destruction, etc.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|17|ב": `And the sanctity of a house of study is more severe, etc. — meaning a public house of study, similar to a synagogue; but an individual who establishes a study hall in his house for his need — some say it does not have so much sanctity, as explained in Orach Chayim siman 151 seif 2; and see further there laws of sanctity of synagogue and house of study.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|21|א": `And some say that even when healthy, etc., it is permitted, and therefore they practiced, etc. — see Kesef Mishneh chapter 3 of Laws of Torah Study, who expanded to reject Rambam's words and expanded to bring proofs for those who permit; and Bach wrote that Beit Yosef too left his words unspecified and did not explain the rejections of Rambam's proofs, nor show the place of proof, etc.; and in truth, even though Beit Yosef did not explain, they wrote in Be'ur in Choshen Mishpat and his conclusion there: the general rule is that whoever has no means to support himself may take his wage to teach, whether from the students themselves or from the community. After the Lord informed us of all this, one may say our Rabbi's intent here is that a person should not cast off the yoke of work to live from people in order to learn, but should learn a trade that supports him; if it suffices, good; if not, he takes his sustenance from the community and there is nothing wrong; even if we say Rambam's view is not so but as appears from his words in Mishnah commentary — behold we hold wherever halachah wavers in your hand, follow the custom; and we saw all sages of Israel before and after our Rabbi practice taking wages from the community; and also it is known halachah is like Rabbenu in Mishnah commentary; it is possible all sages of the generations agreed because of "a time to act for the Lord, they have voided Your Torah," for if sustenance of learners and teachers were not available they could not toil in Torah properly and Torah would be forgotten, Heaven forbid; and when it exists, the community is obligated to support them as they see fit.`,
  "siman_246/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|21|ב": `But he may not accept gifts, etc. — and in Kesef Mishneh there it implies even gifts are permitted to accept; and so Maharshal there, that there is no decisive ruling, and whoever is strict is strict, for it is not written in the verse "and one who takes gifts shall die," etc.; see there. And Bach too expanded that it is permitted for a rosh yeshivah or av beit din to accept gifts until he becomes wealthy, like a nasi and kohen gadol; see there, and so they practiced.`,
  "siman_246/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|17|_": `One does not say to him "heal," etc. — it is written in Perishah: it is possible this applies specifically in their days when they did not show their books outside, all the more so they did not read [by heart]; but now when in any case people are not careful, they say "heal" — end. And he should not have written this to invent a leniency from his heart, for even though we find in Orach Chayim siman 70 a similar idea regarding a groom reading Shema nowadays, since other people too are not careful — that is because a good thing follows, that he will read Shema; which is not so here, where something improper follows, that the listener thinks: since it is now permitted to say "heal," the strap is permitted, and he will also engage in idle talk; and we are already warned of this with punishment of glowing coals, Heaven forbid; therefore one should not heed this to be lenient; on the contrary, the rabbi should warn about this and they will learn a fortiori regarding prohibition of idle talk; and one who increases honor of Torah is honored and praised.`,
  "siman_247/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|2|_": `Rambam's language in chapter 10 of Laws of Gifts to the Poor.`,
  "siman_247/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|3|_": `Shabbat daf 151b.`,
  "siman_247/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3|א": `And he shall give, etc. — as above, and as written (Proverbs 21:13): "He who stops his ear," etc.`,
  "siman_247/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3|ב": `Also he shall give, etc. — Shabbat there.`,
  "siman_247/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4|א": `Charity, etc. — chapter 1 of Bava Batra (10a), and end of Shabbat.`,
  "siman_247/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|4|א": `And in famine it saves from death — and the same applies to other kinds of death, it saves, as in end of Shabbat: how many workers; and it also adds length of days, as in the first chapter of Bava Batra regarding Benyamin the Righteous.`,

  // batch 4 — simanim 257, 270, 295
  "siman_257/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|3|ג": `To inform — Mordechai wrote: where one vowed through the synagogue reader in the synagogue to give charity, since the collector knows he is obligated in charity, he does not transgress until the collector demands him.`,
  "siman_257/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ד": `And when they want to remove him, etc. — Shach and Pri Megadim siman 53 seif 26.`,
  "siman_257/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3|ה": `And likewise if he stipulated, etc. — Rambam; and plainly everything follows the condition; and Pri Megadim siman 254 seif 8.`,
  "siman_257/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5|ג": `One who separates tithes, etc.; and specifically, etc.; and one need not be concerned, etc. — mishnah and Gemara in Gittin chapter 3, page 1: "One who lends," etc., and he need not be concerned, etc.; and there page 2: "The rich, the poor," etc.; except that there we say specifically when selling or transferring to them through another, and so Rambam [and Shach note 13]; and we further say there that if they died he takes permission from the heirs; but Ramban wrote specifically regarding a kohen and levi, but for a poor person it does not help, as in Yerushalmi there: R. Chiya bar Aba in the name of R. Yehoshua ben Levi — regarding heirs of kehunah and levi'ut it is the mishnah, but regarding heirs of a poor person "a poor person has no inheritance"; Bar Kappara taught: there is no person who does not eventually come to this measure, if not he then his son, etc.; and in Sefer HaTerumot end of chapter 65; and we further say in the Gemara there that if he lent through the court he separates on presumption of poor of Israel; and Tosafot there dh "A.B." that specifically on presumption of poor of the city.`,
  "siman_257/nekudot-hakesef/part-001.txt|2|_": `(Siman 257 Taz note 6) He challenged the author for not being correct, for Mahariik's language there is plainly that he could entrust it to another hand, for he is a city partner — everything that comes is by his intent, etc.; and all the more R. Yosef could entrust the purse to whomever he wished, since he could do whatever he wanted with it — this is obvious. And you might say: so too — behold Perishah on one who gives explained that everything depends on her and by her intent he entrusted and gave; if so, she too can entrust to whomever she wishes, etc. — plainly they are not comparable, for there everything that came was by R. Yosef's intent, but here, although she was given authority over the profit from the hundred perutot, nevertheless over the principal she was not given authority at all; and we must say he does not want the deposit of a hundred perutot in another hand besides his wife — end. Behold explicitly he distinguishes in unspecified cases between his wife and a city partner: for a city partner, presumably his intent is to do with it whatever he wishes; but for his wife in unspecified cases we do not say so — this is the author's view, that he deals with unspecified cases; thereby there is a distinction between his wife and a city partner. And what is written in the conclusion "he may entrust to his wife" is a continuation of the language of the beginning, and the same applies to another hand, for Beit Yosef wrote explicitly that a city partner may entrust them to whomever he wishes; and so too Taz explicitly.`,
  "siman_270/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ב": `And likewise for the need of redeeming captives, etc. — Tosafot Bava Batra 5b dh "pidyon," etc.`,
  "siman_270/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 270 seif 1) To write for himself a sefer Torah — see responsum Ranach (part 2 siman 64).`,
  "siman_270/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|2|_": `Nowadays, etc. — Perishah wrote: it appears this is what Rosh meant — the positive mitzvah is said specifically regarding these and not regarding a sefer Torah, for only in their days did they learn Torah by heart not from writing but orally, and needed to learn from a sefer Torah bound properly and precise in missing and extra letters and cantillation notes, etc.; but in our time, when we have permission to write books page by page each separately — why should we belittle the honor of a sefer Torah in vain to learn from it not for need? It follows that in those generations when they do not learn from it, there is no positive mitzvah in them — end; and so appears the main view of Rosh, and not like Beit Yosef and Bach who wrote that Rosh's view is certainly that there is a positive mitzvah to write a sefer Torah even nowadays, except that there is also a mitzvah to write chumashim of Torah, Mishnah, Gemara, and their commentaries; however Taz too and nevertheless Rosh at the beginning of Laws of Sefer Torah followed Beit Yosef's words.`,
  "siman_270/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|א": `It is a positive mitzvah, etc. — as it says "And now, write for yourselves this song," meaning the entire Torah until the end of the song of Ha'azinu, which is the whole Torah; and it is in the Gemara that Tur brought: one who buys a sefer Torah from the market is like one who snatches a mitzvah from the market — he wrote it or corrected in it even one letter, it is as though he received it from Mount Sinai. And Nimukei Yosef explained "like snatching a mitzvah" — he does not have as great reward as one who toiled in writing it; and there is room for strict judgment to distinguish and say: if not for the toil he would not have done it — whether he wrote it himself or hired a scribe to write it and toiled in preparing the parchment and bore the scribe's yoke until it was written — as though he received it, there is room for mercy to say: just as he toiled for this, so he would have toiled to go in the desert to receive from Mount Sinai; even if one letter was mistaken and he corrected it as though he wrote it, there is room for mercy to say: just as he corrected this, so if he found many errors he would correct them — end. And it is in Tosafot in HaKomatz Rabbah: one letter — meaning in a sefer Torah he bought from the market, he is no longer considered like snatching a mitzvah, for it was with his fellow [in sin] who was delaying [a scroll] that was not corrected — I am elevated on this as though he wrote it — end. And Maharshal on this: and according to this, one who buys a sefer Torah from a gentile who took it from an Israelite is not like snatching a mitzvah but as though he wrote it — end. And Rama brought this nearby and wrote that what he wrote "like snatching from the market" and one does not fulfill through this is against Rashi's words, who wrote that if he bought it he did a mitzvah, and if he wrote it it is a greater mitzvah.`,
  "siman_270/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|ג": `In order to buy a new one — Beit Yosef wrote in the name of R. Menachem that the reason is on account of negligence; therefore if the new one is written and is in the scribe's house and lacks only payment of money, they may sell — end. And it is difficult, for in the Gemara this is a question that was not resolved, and strictly; and so Derishah challenged. It appears in my humble view that Beit Yosef in the name of R. Menachem deduced from the Gemara's question: what is the law — may one sell an old sefer Torah to buy a new one, since it does not ascend in sanctity it is forbidden, or perhaps since there is no other elevation, etc.; come and hear: Rabbah bar bar Chana said R. Yochanan in the name of Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel: a person should not sell an old sefer Torah to buy a new one; and there it is rejected on account of negligence (lest after selling he not buy again and the money be lost). When we say for example that it is written and ready to be taken out — what (since it is already in the scribe's house and is not delayed except payment of money) — it implies from this that R. Shimon ben Gamliel's words are plain that he deals on account of negligence; and if so, it was difficult for R. Menachem why he did not bring Rif except R. Shimon ben Gamliel's words and not the questioners regarding Menachem la'aprukei — rather learn that in this doubt we hold leniently, for certainly there is no Torah doubt here; and why be strict in a Gemara doubt here? And it does not seem correct to R. Menachem as Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Ran that Rif's view is to be strict; and so Beit Yosef brought in siman 280 for Rambam who ruled leniently in an unresolved question regarding stitching a sefer Torah; and in Orach Chayim siman 153 the laws of selling a sefer Torah are explained.`,
  "siman_295/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ב": `And likewise one who grafts, etc. — Rambam's language; and he omitted "vegetable with vegetable" because we have a question in Chullin 60: "One who grafted two grasses," etc., and it implies to him even in the Land; and that which we learn in the mishnah in the first chapter "One may not graft," etc. — he holds it is a general prohibition rabbinically [from here one learns that tree with vegetable and vegetable with tree receives lashes]. And Tosafot there dh "One who grafted," etc.; and all this Rosh wrote in the mishnah there. And Rosh in his commentary wrote like the first answer that outside the Land is the question for him; but it appears like the last answer that for Noahides is the question, and so in Yerushalmi chapter 1 of Kilayim: it teaches in the name of R. Eliezer — a gentile is permitted to wear and sow kilayim, but not to crossbreed his animal kilayim nor to graft his tree kilayim; why? Because it is written in them "after their kinds"; but grasses — it is not written in them "after their kinds"; it is not written in the command to Noahides except regarding procreation; and so Tur's view who left unspecified and wrote "between vegetable and vegetable"; see there.`,
  "siman_295/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3|א": `Permitted, etc.; and likewise, etc. — Tosefta chapter 1 of Kilayim: they sow seed and tree seeds together; one who sows grape pits with wheat — behold, he receives forty lashes. And in Yerushalmi chapter 1 of Pe'ah it teaches: they sow seeds and tree seeds together, and one who sows grape pits with wheat receives forty lashes. R. Zeira: it is written "You shall not sow your vineyard kilayim" — the principal of your vineyard you shall not sow kilayim. R. Yudan of Kapudkia asked before R. Yosi: there we say tree seeds are called seeds, and you say tree seeds are not called seeds? He said to him: there Scripture limited that gentiles' way is not to call them seeds; but here Scripture expanded "every seed that is sown"; meaning for purpose of fitness. And so in Gemara Bekhorot 54a: just as "tirosh and yitzhar," etc. — "tirosh and dagan," etc.; as above, wheat and grape pits are liable on account of kilayei hakerem, and like R. Yonatan, not like R. Yoshiah, as written above; and for R. Yoshiah, etc.; and as in Yerushalmi above "the principal of your vineyard," etc., and it is for R. Yonatan; and as written in chapter 8 of Kilayim according to R. Yonatan: it is written "Your field you shall not sow kilayim" — for what matter is it said "You shall not sow your vineyard kilayim," etc., like that which R. Zeira said: "You shall not sow your vineyard kilayim — the principal of your vineyard you shall not sow kilayim"; R. Yudan of Kapudkia asked before R. Yosi: there you say there are no tree seeds, etc., as above; and meaning "the principal of your vineyard" is not like R. Yoshiah who said with kilayei zera'im "You shall not sow your vineyard" — two wheats, etc.; but "the principal," etc., because all trees are permitted with seeds, as above; and that which was asked there "you say," etc.; and Beit HaGadol and so Beit Yosef; but he retracted in dh "vehahu" and it is an error, for there it was stated to the contrary that it is kilayim; see there. And Rosh brought it in chapter 1 mishnah 2 Yerushalmi: R. Yosi, etc.; R. Yosi said, etc.; and so there end of mishnah 7 Yerushalmi: the version of R. Yosi switches, etc.; but there is no difficulty — any two kinds are called kilayim even though they are permitted; and this is what is meant "its principal is kilayim" — meaning of two kinds; and what is written mishnah 7 "switches," etc. — meaning R. Yosi who permits vegetable with tree because they do not crossbreed with each other; and there he said "one removes," etc., and "they join" there, etc. And in Tur it is written: but it is permitted to mix tree seeds with field seeds and sow them together, for the Torah forbade outside the Land only grafting, etc.; and Beit Yosef wrote: it appears from his words that in the Land it is forbidden. Heaven forbid to attribute such an error to him, for the Gemara in Bekhorot above is in the Land, since it says "tirosh and yitzhar" are kilayim, and that is in the Land specifically, as in Kiddushin there and as written in siman 296; rather it is a scribal error and must read "and the Torah did not forbid," etc.; and this is a matter by itself; and so Rosh.`,
  "siman_295/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4|א": `There is prohibition, etc. — these are Tur's words from Rosh in his commentary.`,
  "siman_295/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5|א": `And specifically, etc. — Rosh; and he brought proof from a single vine whose work is 6 tefachim even though the roots spread outside six; and we say: if there was a fence between them, this one leans on the fence from here and that one leans on the fence from here. But Rosh in the first chapter of Kiddushin, brought in Beit Yosef and Beit HaGadol, wrote that vine roots spread downward from 6 tefachim; therefore it is permitted from the side; and so Rosh there — and from the side even for a vine it is permitted, for they do not walk from the side. And Tosafot Kiddushin 39a dh "Lo." And it appears, etc.; and Tosafot's words require study.`,
  "siman_295/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6|ו": `And nevertheless, etc. — Semak and the others of the mishnah, and they are mentioned in Shulchan Aruch — he wrote them.`,

  // batch 5 — simanim 208, 238, 251, 256, 286, 292
  "siman_208/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1|ב": `According to the second explanation that Rosh wrote on what we learn in the mishnah and their explanation is lenient; and so Tur; and so from Rashba's words in a responsum who wrote: if unspecified it is forbidden — meaning if unspecified and he did not know what he intended, it is forbidden.`,
  "siman_208/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|4|_": `(There seif 5) That he shall not marry — see Nekudat HaKesef below (siman 218).`,
  "siman_208/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|5|א": `He swore that he shall not marry, etc. — and the oath takes effect in such a case even though he nullifies the mitzvah of procreation, as below siman 239 seif 8 in the gloss; and see there what I wrote in the name of Or Zaru'a that the oath does not take effect; and according to this one may be lenient here that he need not divorce her.`,
  "siman_208/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|_": `And if most people of the place, etc. — Beit Yosef challenged from what is written in siman 217: one who vows from oil — if they are uncertain between the two, both are forbidden even though most of their supply is from one of them; and he answered that there everyone is supplied from both, except that most of their supply is from one — therefore since everyone is supplied from both, both are forbidden — end. And his words are astounding, for if we heed majority here too we should heed majority, and we do not follow the minority of people's supply. And in truth one may further challenge this case here where we follow majority, and so in siman 280 seif 14: if the place he vowed in is a barley harvest we follow it, even leniently; and in siman 217 seif 3 he wrote regarding doubt what is called "pickled" that we do not follow majority in vows but say to be strict. And Maharach wrote in siman 280 to resolve that case in siman 208 regarding salted meat of consecrated animals that we follow majority because the majority call it "salted" unspecified; and so in siman 280 regarding harvest — the majority call wheat harvest and barley harvest "harvest" unspecified; but regarding oil in siman 217 where we do not follow majority, it deals where both are called "oil" unspecified except that the majority are supplied from one of them. And he omitted that case in siman 217 seif 3 regarding pickled where we say there: some people of the place call it thus and some do not call — we do not follow majority but this is a vow doubt, and every vow doubt is strict; behold this is the main strictness that we do not follow the majority's terminology. The challenge returns to its place; and it appears in my humble view that all is resolved: certainly wherever there is doubt in the forbidden item itself we go strict and do not follow majority — this is the vow strictness that whatever can enter prohibition is forbidden even if a minority in that name; but if the doubt is not in the forbidden item itself but he attached a sign, then we interpret that sign according to common majority language; and since he came to give a sign and explain his words, he certainly intended something clear, for a person does not put himself in doubt; therefore in that case in siman 217 regarding oil and also regarding pickled, which is the forbidden item itself, we go strict even after minority; not so here where he attached these fruits to salted meat, and so in siman 280 regarding harvest — he says this thing shall be forbidden to me until the harvest; behold the doubt is in what he attached the forbidden item to, and he came to clarify what he attached it to — then we say he intended something clear, namely the majority; and clear proof from Kiddushin chapter HaOmer (64a): one who has two groups of daughters and said "I betrothed my elder daughter" and does not know whether elder among elders or elder among younger — R. Yosi says all are permitted except the elder among elders, and we hold like him; we explain the reason there that a person does not put himself in doubt — Rashi: he would need to ask which she is; and we say there that regarding vows too it is so — behold whenever one comes to give a sign to his words, i.e. says "the elder," we say a person does not put himself in doubt; whereas if he said only "my daughter is betrothed" unspecified, all are in doubt and we do not say he certainly intended the elder since most of the world marry the elder first; and even though Rosh wrote in chapter HaIsh Mekadesh, etc., and Rabbenu Tam said perhaps we do not say they are not given to relations except "one of your daughters," but "your daughter is betrothed to me" implies specifically the elder — he did not say so for practical halachah — end; it implies for halachah without action he would hold even for "your daughter is betrothed to me" he intended the elder; but there that argument of Rabbenu Tam is strong, for a person does not marry the younger before the elder since Scripture says "so shall not be done in our place," etc., and it is as though he said explicitly "the elder," as Rosh wrote there in his name — if she is betrothed to him he certainly intended her; but in other places where we have no strong presumption before us to which he intended, we certainly go strict even after minority; but if he came to clarify his words and attached a sign, then we follow majority; and so this case here appears correct and clear in my humble view; and I saw others write very forced things on this — what I wrote is clear with Heaven's help.`,
  "siman_238/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|_": `An oath, etc. — Rambam and Rosh [and so Rosh there siman 65]; and like all prohibitions forbidden by Torah according to R. Yochanan in Yoma 74a; and even though here the prohibition comes through himself, one may say he did not intend less than that; and as written — dispute in unspecified; but in specified, etc.; nevertheless since in Yoma there we say because it can combine, so here not like what Rosh wrote in his novellae.`,
  "siman_238/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|7|_": `An oath that he shall not eat, or, etc. — Rif and Rambam; but according to Rashi's view specifically in unspecified, as written at the beginning "in unspecified," etc., "in specified," etc., except like Rava, etc.; but Rosh wrote that in all books the version is found like Rava, and do not read "except"; and further we learn in Rava: oath "I shall eat" and he ate dirt — exempt; and Rosh explained: meaning since you find them in "I shall eat" unspecified it suffices, and he is liable for the prohibition even in specified, as in Tosefta that Tosafot brought there dh "except oath I shall eat," etc.; and you might ask why exempt — behold we say at the beginning of the mishnah "oath I shall not eat" and he ate foods that are not, etc., that nevelah is not included — answer: one who swears "I shall not eat" does not intend except fit things, for unfit things one need not swear on; but "I shall eat" his intent is on everything he will eat — he fulfills through this like Rava who said, etc.; and even though if he swore "I shall not eat" he is exempt if he ate dirt, as written at the beginning "and he ate unfit foods" — dirt too is unfit, as Rava himself said there "oath I shall not eat," etc.; and this is Rif's and the Geonim's view, as there page 2; and R. A. to one who says to him, etc.; and there "and if it were so you would find," etc.; and Tosafot there dh "that," etc., strained themselves on this; and so there page 1 dh "except." However one may say, etc.; and Rosh (in his novellae) answered one answer; but according to the Geonim all is resolved.`,
  "siman_238/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|8|_": `(Shach note 26) And so in responsum Maharam Mintz. And he deduced thus from Rashi's language: since a sage uproots the vow from its root; but in my humble view it is not proof, for Rosh wrote in Nedarim (8a) that one who swore to fulfill the mitzvah takes effect for the prohibition of "he shall not profane," but not for a sacrifice, for we exclude from "to do evil or good"; however this is specifically one who swore to fulfill a positive mitzvah like sitting in a sukkah and the like; but one who swore to fulfill a negative mitzvah such as not eating nevelah — certainly it does not take effect even for "he shall not profane," as explicitly in the sugya in Makkot (22a) where we challenge there and consider also an oath not to plow on a festival, and answer there the oath does not take effect because he is already sworn from Sinai; and there it deals entirely regarding lashes, and therefore the reason is from "a prohibition does not take effect on a prohibition" as generally; and automatically even if there is no prohibition; but one who swore to fulfill a positive mitzvah where there is no "a prohibition does not take effect on a prohibition" in this — Rosh wrote it takes effect for the prohibition of "he shall not profane"; and similarly Tosafot Shevuot (27a) on R. Yehuda ben Beteira who obligates one who swore to fulfill and did not fulfill — he holds one need not be both prohibition and affirmation; nevertheless he agrees regarding one who swore not to eat nevelah because of "a prohibition does not take effect"; see there; and likewise for us who hold like the first tanna that one who swore to fulfill and did not fulfill is exempt because there is no prohibition and affirmation — that is regarding sacrifice; but regarding lashes there is a distinction between fulfilling a positive mitzvah and fulfilling a negative mitzvah — and this is clear in Rosh's intent. Accordingly one may say that what Rashi wrote — the reason a sage uproots a vow from its root — is that Rava's language "if he was asked about the first, the second rose for him in its place" implies the second has full law of an oath; and thereby it is difficult — behold it is called "swore to fulfill the mitzvah" — the first oath, and there is no sacrifice [and especially why Rashi wrote in Makkot regarding oath not to plow that it does not take effect — we exclude from "to do evil or good" — it is clear to explain Rashi that also for the prohibition we exclude from "to do evil or good," not like Rosh above]; therefore the reason a sage uproots from its root is needed and it is not in the category of "swore to fulfill." And in truth if the husband annulled the first, she is not liable for a sacrifice on the second; but nevertheless for the prohibition of "he shall not profane," which we do not exclude by verse, and we come to judge only from "a prohibition does not take effect," one may say it is like generally that it hangs and stands — if the first falls, the second takes effect, as in Yevamot (32a); and even with husband's annulment the second takes effect for the prohibition since the first fell. And analyze well. Further it appears to me there is no proof from Rashi's words — one may say it implies the second rose for him in its place meaning if he ate before being asked about the first, he transgressed retroactively with this eating on the second, like the case of two nezirut; and even though the conclusion of the sugya is Rava, and he holds there if he ate it all he is not asked about it — nevertheless we need not ask regarding "oath I shall eat" "I shall not eat" — there is a difference if he ate an olive-bulk and was asked, he is liable retroactively on that olive because of the second; and even in "I shall not eat" "I shall not eat it" — if for one who ate an olive-bulk he was asked and afterward ate the remainder — if we say he did not transgress on the second with the first olive he ate before being asked, automatically he would not be liable for the eating after being asked, for he did not eat it all in prohibition; and on this Rashi came to give the reason a sage uproots from its root and he transgresses retroactively on the second; in such a case with husband's annulment it does not take effect; but what he ate after annulment one may say it takes effect as generally — when he finds an opening it takes effect; analyze.`,
  "siman_238/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|12|_": `The second does not take effect — and behold nine and ten unspecified are a vow; if so he cannot eat ten without there being nine among them that he first vowed on; and in every ten he eats one may say he swore on nine of these in the first oath — automatically there are here two oaths on nine, and an oath does not take effect on an oath; not so when he says "these nine" and an oath that he shall not eat ten unspecified — for those ten unspecified cannot contain these nine he first swore on, since he swore on these nine; it follows if he would eat these nine and ten unspecified, plainly he would be liable for two; therefore if he eats these nine and one unspecified, this one can combine with the nine unspecified — if he would eat another nine unspecified he would be liable on them because of the oath; and since the one unspecified takes effect, these nine too take effect, that this one combines with these nine so that he transgresses two oaths — one on ten and also he already has an oath on these nine — he transgresses two oaths, and it is similar to one who swore not to eat figs and again swore not to eat figs and grapes — he is liable twice on figs; and it is possible if he ate these nine and ten unspecified he is liable for three oaths, similar to figs and grapes — if he also ate the grapes, plainly he is liable three — two on figs and one on grapes. Perishah.`,
  "siman_251/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|9|_": `Mishnah 5 chapter 1 of Avot and Bava Metzia daf 60b.`,
  "siman_251/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|א": `One who is, etc. — Semag's language, meaning "apostate for appetite," as written; and what is written in chapter 4 of Gittin (47a): R. Ami wanted to redeem him — beforehand because he is a criminal he is not your brother and there is no mitzvah of "from one of your brothers" and "and your brother shall live with you" until he is lashed, as in Makkot 23a in the mishnah; and so Semak; and as written in seif 2; and so Beit Yosef deduced from Rambam's language who wrote in seif 2 "one who is a criminal to provoke," etc. — implies for appetite there is no prohibition; but also no obligation; and as in the gloss "but criminal," etc.; and it is not forced; and Tosafot Avodah Zarah 26b dh "Ani" wrote they are obligated to redeem and lend him — and so is the main view.`,
  "siman_251/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|3|א": `And likewise one who gives, etc. — Tosafot Kiddushin 32a dh "Oru," etc.`,
  "siman_251/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2|_": `Forbidden to redeem him — in Tur it is written regarding a poor person who transgresses one of all the mitzvot, etc., he is not obligated to give him charity, etc.; and it deals with to provoke, for for appetite he mentions afterward in the conclusion; therefore necessarily to provoke; and Beit Yosef challenged that from the language "he is not obligated" it implies there is no prohibition if he gives him; and the case of R. Ami is clear that it is forbidden to sustain him; and Perishah answered that for provoking once he is not called apostate and there is no prohibition to sustain him; and so an apostate who does many times, even for appetite it is so; but an apostate to provoke — meaning many times — there is prohibition to sustain him; and it is not clear to my humble view that also the first time when he acts to provoke there is prohibition, as implied by Rambam's language that Beit Yosef brings, who wrote he would eat nevelah to provoke; and so Tur's language siman 252; and nevertheless there is prohibition to redeem him, and it implies once; and so proven in Gemara in chapter HaSholeach where it says "sometimes there is permitted and forbidden before him and he leaves the permitted," etc. — language "times" implies once (in chapter HaKotev Ketubot 85a we say "times R. Papa sat before him," etc. — meaning once, as proven there); and what Perishah wrote that once he is not called apostate is true, for so Tosafot in the first chapter of Chullin for one answer in the mishnah "one who slaughters on Shabbat and Yom Kippur," etc., is not relevant here, for there it deals whether his slaughter is disqualified as slaughter of an apostate to desecrate Shabbat — we might say he is apostate to the whole Torah; and on this we say because of, etc. — end.`,
  "siman_256/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1|_": `Rambam's language at the beginning of chapter 9 of Laws of Positive Mitzvot, from the implication of the Gemara Bava Batra 8, and from mishnah at end of Pe'ah; and so Mordechai in the first chapter of Bava Batra in the name of R. Meir — the same applies to bringing in guests and distributing charity to them.`,
  "siman_256/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4|ה": `And likewise if he specified, etc. — from what is written in chapter 8 of Bava Kamma: the poor of Pumbedita finished, etc.; and Rambam wrote "and already acquired by them," etc. — plainly one may not change; there; and in Tosefta that Rashbam brought in Bava Batra 43b, and Rosh there siman 66: "One who says give," etc.`,
  "siman_256/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6|א": `And that they must give because of suspicion — Rashi dh "they give," etc.; and this is what is written "and specifically," etc.`,
  "siman_256/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|4|_": `They may not change even for Torah study — in Mahariwe siman 27 he explained the reason: although elsewhere it is permitted to change, nevertheless here where he says they should give his money to the poor for a weekly allowance, the poor of the city already acquired it; and he brings proof from what Rambam wrote chapter 5 of Laws of Guardians: but if he said to these poor or these captives, behold it is acquired by them and it is money that has claimants — end; and see siman 259 seif 6 where I proved that Tur's language in the name of Rosh — that they may change it even for optional matters — is specifically regarding fund and tamchui that is a fixed matter and if they lack they will collect again; but if a case occurred that they needed to collect for the poor, such as for clothing need or poor came and they collected for their name, they may not change for another purpose even for other poor — end. Maharik wrote in root 7: two who agreed to give equally to charity and captives occurred to them and one wants to collect according to wealth and the other claims they already agreed to give equally to charity and redeeming captives is also included in charity — he ruled one must compromise whether it is included in language of charity unspecified; nevertheless in people's language it is not included; and charity is a vow and in vows we follow people's language, for in people's language they call charity the needs of the poor — food, drink, clothing and the like; and from this he derived another law that Rama brought siman 252 seif 1 regarding one who says, etc.`,
  "siman_286/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|20|_": `Behind — Rashi explained: for example there are in the corner of the house fixed doorposts for two doorways cut in it, one to the north and one to the west; and they fixed the mezuzah in the doorposts in the northern doorway's opening; and when the door is open, the back of the door — if in the thickness of the doorposts there is a separation of a tefach between doorway and doorway, another mezuzah is needed for the western doorway since the mezuzah is not in its opening; and if not, no other mezuzah is needed since it is as though placed in its own opening — end; and it appears from here that those doorways that are divided with a pillar in the middle suffice with one mezuzah even if the pillar's thickness is a tefach — one is not obligated in two except when one is north and one west; but when both doorways are in one direction, even if the doorposts' thickness is a tefach, all is considered one doorway. And Taz explains Rashi's words: meaning both doorways are cut in the doorposts themselves — in the northwest corner stands one thick wide doorpost cut with a large cut for doorway measure from the north side into the house's opening, and there they enter and exit from north to house and house to north; and another doorway measure is cut from the west side to the house's opening, and there is entry and exit from house to west and west to house; and he made the mezuzah in the western doorway's opening — on the side that is right for one entering from west to house; and thereby the mezuzah of the western doorway is plainly as law; but for the northern doorway there is a distinction if in that part of doorposts separating west from north there is a tefach measure, etc.`,
  "siman_286/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|9|_": `A house, etc. — see Rashi there dh "and the halachah," etc., and Rosh there.`,
  "siman_286/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|18|א": `Since, etc. — according to the second explanation in Rashi above in siman 17; but Shulchan Aruch copied Rambam's language who explains from what is written "follow the regular" — meaning the doorway between him and the house of study; see there.`,
  "siman_286/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|22|_": `And likewise, etc. — as written in Tanhuma, etc.; (Addition) And likewise, etc. — as in Shabbat 131b: since he can renounce them, meaning even to dwell in them and he dwells in them he is exempt, for a borrowed cloak is exempt, and so a borrowed house; and one need not force that he holds vessels in a box are obligated in tzitzit, for regarding his cloak and doorway he spoke unspecifiedly implying in all cases forbidden (see Rashba in Shabbat there and Tosafot Menachot 44a dh "cloak" — requires study); and that which we say in the first chapter of Rosh Hashanah "but your house" — why? It is to exclude one who dwells in a house that is not his, as written there regarding tzitzit "but your garment" — why? etc.; rather one of two reasons he cited (Rashba in Shabbat there and Tosafot Avodah Zarah 21a dh "Ha." And even though we expound, etc.; and in Menachot 44a dh "cloak," etc.) (end).`,
  "siman_292/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|3|ד": `Rambam in chapter 13 of Laws of Shechitah law 17.`,
  "siman_292/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_": `Sending away the mother bird — see responsum Chavot Yair siman 67: they asked if when a bird's nest happens before a man in the field he is obligated at least to attend to send the mother, or may go his way — for Torah did not say "send, you shall send" except if he wanted to take the chicks; and he wrote that from Tosafot's words on the question of two rows of eggs one atop the other, brought in Shach note 14, it is proven he is not obligated to attend; but Tosafot's words are not compelled, for Rashba's explanation appears more correct; and from Gemara's words it implies he is obligated to attend, for it says "perhaps he will return to the mountains," etc. — "when it happens" implies it falls on him to attend, especially according to Zohar's reason, etc.; see there; and see in Mishnat Chachamim beginning Laws of Foundations of Torah Tzafnat Pa'aneach there letter he who wrote: just as they said regarding tzitzit there is no punishment except at time of divine wrath, so here if one sees a nest and does not attend to send the mother he is punished at time of wrath; but there is no punishment like one who transgresses a positive mitzvah incumbent on him — positive mitzvah means if he took the mother and chicks; see there.`,
  "siman_292/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|3|_": `Two rows of eggs — Shach note 14; and see responsum Chavot Yair siman 67 what he wrote on this.`,
  "siman_292/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|9|_": `Two rows of eggs, etc. — Taz wrote he intended to take only the lower ones alone — and this is like Rashi's explanation; but in Rashba's novellae it is written it is not clear, for in any case the mother broods on the upper eggs; and even if he takes neither upper nor lower — we learn (and it is explained in seif 5) "I will take the mother and send away the chicks" — he said nothing; except here when the upper are eggs of impure fowl, or alternatively with ready eggs similar to a cloak, such as he put his hand to the nest and placed his eggs there to make separation between the mother and eggs — so it appears to him — end; and to me the plain meaning of the Gemara is main like Rashi, and so plain from Tur's, Rambam's and the author's words; and there is no difficulty from what we learn "I will take the mother," etc. — for here he does not want to take the mother but only the lower eggs alone; and the Gemara's question is whether the upper eggs are separation — if so these lower eggs are as though placed elsewhere and permitted to take before sending; or not separation and forbidden to take them; but the mother in any case is forbidden to take because she broods on the upper eggs even though he does not intend to take them; and it appears this is Tosafot's view and not like Sefer Torat Chaim who wondered on Rashi and Tosafot; and according to what I wrote for preliminary understanding, see there — so it appears to me.`,

  // batch 6 — simanim 210, 212, 220, 224, 227, 233
  "siman_210/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1|_": `The statement of Shmuel and the baraita, as explained there in the Gemara Shevuot 26. (°) And Rosh explained: he is not forbidden regarding wheat, for he did not utter it with his lips; nor regarding barley bread, for he did not intend to say barley — unlike when he intended wheat and uttered "bread" unspecified, for wheat bread is called "bread."`,
  "siman_210/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ג": `However, etc. — so too Rashba, not like the Geonim; and his proof from what is written in chapter HaRo'eh (55b): one who sees a dream, etc., "they will treat him well before three." And all the more so according to Rambam's version that even for "they excommunicated him" it suffices thus, as written above siman 334 seif 35.`,
  "siman_210/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|3|_": `That they release him — Beit HaTov in the name of Shach; and see responsum Perach Shoshan Yoreh De'ah general 3 siman 9 regarding one who vowed in a dream and retracted on the 24th — whether we say retraction is retraction or not; and he wrote that according to Shach who wants to learn from excommunication to vows — even though they released him in the dream, release is required — so too here retraction on the 24th does not help since we are concerned retraction is idle words; but in truth it appears one should not learn from excommunication to vows, for only regarding excommunication, which is from heaven, they were so strict; not so regarding vows where there is an opinion that no release is needed at all — do not add to it, etc.; see there. [And see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 222 regarding one who dreamed he vowed not to eat boiled matzah this Passover until Rosh Hashanah, and the questioner was in doubt on three matters — answered on the first doubt that certainly he is forbidden only on the first Passover before us; and that "this" is an idle matter, etc.; see there at length.]`,
  "siman_212/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|א": `One who vows, etc. — Rambam's language; and he explains it refers to actual "I vow"; and Raavad brought proof from chapter 12 of Laws of Shevuot from Yerushalmi that Rosh brings there: in Torah it is permitted like the sanctity of Torah — meaning as written, his intent is on the scrolls that were sanctified through the letters; and here there is no vowed matter in "something in which prohibition is written" like sacrifices written therein.`,
  "siman_212/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_": `One who swears by the Torah — see responsum Har HaCarmel Yoreh De'ah siman 21 regarding one who was required to swear on a sefer Torah missing a word, letter, or sheet — whether for this reason it is not considered swearing on a sefer Torah; and also whether one may initially omit a sheet, word, or letters; and he wrote that even if his act would help, nevertheless it is forbidden to do so initially to erase letters, and all the more to omit a sheet from a sefer Torah; but in truth it does not help, for nevertheless it is properly called swearing on a sefer Torah; and also one should not rely on swearing on haftarot since it is written in Assyrian script with ink — even if he mentions orally "I swear on this sefer Torah," he properly says so, for prophets' words are called "book" and called "Torah," and all the more when he holds an actual sefer Torah except missing a sheet or verse — it is called sefer Torah; even if he removes the Ten Commandments and swears "I swear on the Ten Commandments," it is also called an oath — see there; and see responsum Noda B'Yehudah Yoreh De'ah siman 71 on whether one may swear falsely if the sefer Torah he holds is invalid — he answered at length with many proofs that it is forbidden to swear falsely even without holding a book; and the custom to give a book to the one swearing is only to intimidate him and remind him of the oath's punishment in Torah, and not specifically sefer Torah but any books containing divine names — see there at length.`,
  "siman_212/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|ב": `But one who swears by the Torah, etc. — this is Rambam's view that in this seif are his words; but according to Tosafot's and Rosh's explanation and Tur, also for one who swears one may say his intent is on the scrolls, as explained in Tur and Beit Yosef; nevertheless Beit Yosef challenged that he saw in his generation some people swearing by the Torah falsely and excusing themselves from "one who vows by the Torah said nothing," for his intent is scrolls — and he called them "they are for evil"; and it appears one who is obligated to swear should not be given an oath in this language "I swear on the sefer Torah," as I saw some judges do, lest he come to release him and say his intent is scrolls — unless he places his hand on it, then his law is like one holding it in his hand, as mentioned here — so it appears to me.`,
  "siman_220/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|3|א": `Without — Bach ruled that for "this week" and "this month" too one needs questioning at the end of the week and month; see there. And Shach wrote: even if he stood on Shabbat and vowed "this week," he is forbidden only on that Shabbat alone and tomorrow is permitted immediately; and so for month; and below seif 6 regarding year; and so Maharal ibn Chaviv — end. And so if he said "this Shabbat," its law is like saying "this week"; and the same for "Konam wine that I taste this week" or month or year — its law is like "this week," "this month," or "this year"; and he wrote in responsum Mahari Betzalel that likewise if he said "this year" it means until Rosh Hashanah, and it is plain; and he further wrote likewise if he said "until a year" its law is like "this year," and it is not forced — end Shach. And Taz wrote: it appears if he said in Yiddish "diese woch" where Shabbat is not included in the prohibition; but if he said secular language "I will not eat meat," the five or six weekdays — then certainly Shabbat is included since he spoke by counting; but one who says "diese woch" does not intend by day count but only that the days are weekday not Shabbat — and it seems the common custom is so; and all the more if standing on Shabbat he said he forbids himself "diese woch," he certainly did not intend that day but the coming week — end. [And Nekudat HaKesef challenged: if he says "diese woch" it implies Shabbat too since the world calls the seven days "diese woch," so Shabbat is included; and what he wrote "custom seems so" is if he accepted not to eat meat on weekdays only — see there.]`,
  "siman_220/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|3|א": `Or before it — or afterward; and even if he stood on Shabbat and vowed "this week," he is forbidden only on that Shabbat alone and tomorrow is permitted immediately; and so for month, as below seif 6 regarding year; and so wrote Maharal ibn Chaviv there.`,
  "siman_220/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|3|ב": `This week — or "this Shabbat," as in the Gemara that it too is a week.`,
  "siman_224/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1|ב": `Division — meaning in the synagogue and the like there is no law of division, it is permitted; and so appears main in the Gemara — Shach; but Taz wrote that in the Gemara it implies in the synagogue there is law of division and it is forbidden, like the first opinion the author wrote; see there and see Orach Chayim siman 153 seif 17.`,
  "siman_224/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|א": `Or behold, etc. — there 46a in the Gemara; and learn from there.`,
  "siman_224/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_": `And some say they are permitted — Taz and Shach; and see responsum Mishkenot Yaakov siman 48 what he wrote on this.`,
  "siman_227/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 227 seif 1) And sells to them equal for equal. And in Beit Yosef: even in sharp sale it is forbidden, for it is seller's benefit, as stated (in Ketubot)[in Pesachim 113]: "all matters of buying — except wine — he may regret"; see Taz Choshen Mishpat (siman 72 seif 40).`,
  "siman_227/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2|_": `Equal for equal — Tur wrote in the name of Rambam: we do not decree against buying lest he sell (meaning as we decree in siman 221 seif 5) — for there if he does not trade with this one he will trade with another; but here he cannot.`,
  "siman_227/yad-avraham/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 227) See Biur HaGRA who wondered on Rif and Rambam whose words contradict each other — they ruled benefit of use is not money, yet ruled movable property is acquired through land. This difficulty does not revolve on them, for Sha'agat Aryeh already wondered thus on the tanna R. Yehoshua in siman 77; but one may say according to Shach in Choshen Mishpat siman 203 that benefit of use transfers through land even according to the view that it is not money. And again I found he answered thus in Or Chadash on Pesachim, and so too in Machaneh Ephraim; and even though Ketzot HaChoshen challenged Shach, I wrote to resolve in my Chiddushei Choshen Mishpat.`,
  "siman_233/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ד": `(Addition) And thirteen, etc. — even though he did not bring a basket. Rambam; and so implied from Shulchan Aruch's unspecified language referring to what was written above "even though," etc.; but Lechem Mishneh and Magen Avraham wondered on him; and so Raavad in the mishnah there, Taz, and Pri Chadash (end).`,
  "siman_233/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_": `Even though they do not know — Beit HaGadol; and see in Lechem Mishneh and Magen Avraham chapter 1 of Laws of Vows; and see responsum Mishkenot Yaakov siman 57 what he wrote on this.`,
  "siman_233/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 233 Shach note 2) Even though they did not bring a basket — so Rambam; but I wonder, for we explicitly say in the sugya in Niddah (45b): if he did not bring a basket, he is a minor; afterward I saw Magen Avraham wondered thus; however for practical law it appears that even if he did not bring a basket, his vow is a vow — for we are concerned perhaps they fell, as we hold in Shulchan Aruch Even HaEzer (siman 155 seif 2); and that which we challenge in the sugya "if he did not bring a basket he is a minor" — only means he did bring, and the reason is for afterward, etc., lest perhaps he did not bring and afterward we are concerned they fell; but within his time we are not concerned they fell; one must say that since the mishnah teaches "their vow is a vow and their consecration is consecration," it implies it is certainly a vow, and if others ate they are lashed for it — and if he did not bring, it is only a doubt that perhaps they fell — so it appears to me.`,

  // batch 7 — simanim 244, 248, 254, 279, 288
  "siman_244/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|17|_": `Their father — Taz explained that their father has permission to take their sons with them at the time of their entry and to keep their sons with them in their place of residence; and they further said in the Gemara that even at a feast they seat the sons beside the elders because of honor of their father — end.`,
  "siman_244/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|3|_": `One does not stand before them — Lechem Mishneh wrote in chapter 6 of Laws of Torah Study that this law applies even to an outstanding teacher; and see in Arba'ah Turim Even on Rambam there who disagrees and holds that specifically for a teacher who is not outstanding, or for another sage, or for an elder, these laws apply; but for an outstanding teacher who is included in "fear," one must stand before him even in the bathhouse, and so for one's father; and that craftsmen are not obligated or permitted to stand — that is specifically before a sage or a teacher who is not outstanding, but before an outstanding teacher they must stand; therefore Rambam did not bring these laws in the earlier chapter that deals with an outstanding teacher; and so Tur and Shulchan Aruch wrote these laws in siman 244 and not in siman 242 — see there.`,
  "siman_244/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|4|_": `He may waive — Beit HaTov of Mahariit; and see responsum Chavot Yair siman 202: they asked regarding a Torah scholar who knows to play several kinds of musical instruments — whether he may play a harp before bride and groom, or it is forbidden to degrade himself for honor of his Torah and he cannot waive his degradation; and he wrote that apparently this depends on the dispute between Rambam and Rosh regarding an elder for whom it is not his honor, brought in Choshen Mishpat siman 263 seif 3 — according to Rosh there, that even if he wants to be strict he may not, so too here; and he further wrote that in this all permit and it is a mitzvah, since everyone knows he has no reward for his toil and he acts for Heaven's sake — for Rosh's words apply only to a mitzvah between man and fellow, where Torah exempted the elder and it is not for his honor; unlike the general principle "whoever was occupied with his lost object and claims likewise regarding his fellow's" — Torah commanded only "love your neighbor as yourself," and if he wants to be strict he degrades his Torah to occupy with his own money which one should not degrade; but for mitzvot between Israel and the Omnipresent, all the more a time-caused mitzvah — there is no degradation of elder's or Torah's honor; and whoever conducts himself lightly for a lofty purpose and God's mitzvah, even if not literally a positive commandment but only kindness we learn from "walk in His ways," is praiseworthy and blessing comes upon him — see there.`,
  "siman_248/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|4|_": `To accept — and a fine that was fined on her, her husband is obligated to pay on her behalf, as the Rav wrote in Even HaEzer siman 92 seif 4 — and this is from siman 282; and the reason is it is like atonement — Shach.`,
  "siman_248/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|7|_": `Rambam's language there in chapter 7 from the statement of R. Yitzchak bar Shmuel, etc., from the verse "and I will visit upon all who oppress him," as established there in the Gemara Bava Batra 8b.`,
  "siman_248/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|4|_": `Forbidden to accept from them — and a fine fined on her, her husband is obligated to pay on her behalf, as the Rav wrote in Even HaEzer siman 92 seif 4 — and this is from siman 282; and he wrote there the reason is it is like atonement; and he brings it in Derishah siman 7.`,
  "siman_254/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|_": `Forbidden for an Israelite, etc. — because there is desecration of God's name, for he degrades himself in public.`,
  "siman_254/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2|א": `And it shall be given to poor gentiles — but not to poor Israelites, because it is written "in the withering of her harvest they shall be broken" — meaning when the moist merit in gentiles' hands dries up and their charity withers, then they shall be broken. It is difficult: at the beginning they permitted receiving in private and we do not care that the gentile gains merit thereby; and Derishah challenged this and answered that an individual who receives, since he benefits, is permitted; but a gabbai who has no benefit may not cause merit for gentiles for others' benefit — and this is astounding, for how do we permit him to prolong exile for his benefit? It appears to me that "in the withering of her harvest" applies only when the gentile intends specifically for poor Israelites — thereby Israel's name is exalted and there is great merit, as here and like the incident of the mother of King Shapur in the Gemara who sent to divide for Israel; and although Rashi wrote she also knew they sustain poor gentiles with Israel, for otherwise there would be theft of mind as we cite below — nevertheless the main point was for poor Israel, and therefore they were concerned about "withering of harvest"; not so at the beginning where the gentile does not intend specifically on Israel but from his nature is merciful to all who stretch out a hand — this is not great merit and there is no "withering of harvest" — so appears main. And they give to poor gentiles in secret. One may say on what I wrote that the gentile's main intent was for Israel: here he gives everything to poor gentiles and it would be theft of mind — one may answer that theft of mind applies only when the giver intends to give to Israel and not to gentiles; giving to gentiles passes through the gentile's mind who was given a burden to give to gentiles; but where he gives nothing to Israel, there is no theft of mind nor such prohibition.`,
  "siman_254/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2|ב": `And some say one should do with them, etc. — I have not reached the end of Rama's view; his words are astounding, for in the Gemara first chapter of Bava Batra there are two incidents: one on page 10 — the mother of King Shapur sent four hundred zuz to Rava, he accepted them for peace of the kingdom, and divided them for poor gentiles — this is the seif Tur and Shulchan Aruch mention; and also on page 8 — Hormiz sent a purse of dinars to R. Yosef for a great mitzvah, and R. Yosef gave them for redeeming captives, which is a great mitzvah; and Rashi and Tosafot wrote in R. Yosef's incident he did not heed "withering of harvest" because he sent for redeeming captives and could not divide them — therefore he had to give to captive Israelites, for if to captive gentiles he would transgress theft of mind; but regarding Rava who was sent money and could divide for poor gentiles, there is no theft of mind, for they too know Israelites sustain poor gentiles with poor Israel. It follows that if sent for captives one must fulfill the giver's statement because of theft of mind; but money divided for the poor one gives to poor gentiles; and Beit Yosef wrote that since poskim did not divide on this, learn that R. Yosef too divided for gentile captives — meaning "withering of harvest" applies everywhere even for captives; and so Tosafot for one answer on page 8. Therefore Rama's words that one should do as the ruler commanded apply when sent for captives or a great mitzvah like redeeming captives; but money sent for charity one gives to poor gentiles. And in the incident of Darva and Hormiz creation of a company — but for captives. And in Perishah I saw the entire Beit Yosef does not bring to you that if the gentile says stars in a manner that does not belong to gentiles, he says because of theft of mind; and from any difficult place he is against disobeying verses Beit Yosef wrote, who is here to steal the mind — and there is a Taz.`,
  "siman_279/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|2|_": `Beit Yosef in the name of his teacher the Mahari ibn Rav, to resolve Rambam's words that he wrote in a responsum that one blesses on a invalid scroll — meaning for public reading which is an enactment of prophets and Ezra; and what he wrote in his composition to the contrary is regarding fulfilling the positive mitzvah to write only one sefer Torah; and since so, one may rely on him post facto; and so Rashbatz wrote in the name of Ran.`,
  "siman_279/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|4|_": `Rambam's language in chapter 7 of Laws of Sefer Torah, from the baraita Menachot 29b, as explained there. (°) Explanation: Ketuvim as established.`,
  "siman_279/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_": `That is not corrected — see responsum Yad Eliyahu siman 88: a sefer Torah in which one error was found became mixed with others and it is impossible to find the error because it is nullified in the majority, and it is permitted to read from each one; and we do not say it is a significant matter, etc.; and especially since many permit when an error is found — but we do not practice so; and there is also a doubt that perhaps the error is in the other chumash that is not read now — that in time of pressure it is permitted, one may permit here even not under pressure; however because of "do not let wrong dwell in your tent" one must be diligent to search with all one's strength until the error is found — see there. [And see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 277 regarding a sefer Torah in which an error was found, displayed in the ark from the side, forgotten, and again lent to townsmen one sefer Torah from the ark and afterward returned and they said an error was found — now the congregation forgot and do not know if this is the same erroneous sefer Torah or another, and now all scrolls are mixed except this one in which the error is now found; he expanded on this and brought Yad Eliyahu and also Sefer Bnei Yissaschar who wrote in this siman: a sefer Torah in which an error was found and became mixed — initially one must search search after search until the error is found; if he searched and did not find, one may permit because of a double doubt — perhaps this is not the invalid sefer Torah; and if you say it is, perhaps it is not in the chumash we read today; and even if only one became mixed with one, it is also permitted because of double doubt — see there at length.]`,
  "siman_288/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4|ב": `Even though, etc. — Rambam, and not like Tosafot there 32a dh "perhaps," etc.`,
  "siman_288/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6|א": `He wrote it, etc. — Shabbat 79b like Rav; and from the challenge there "mezuzah on a hide," etc., and it does not also say on a scroll — learn that on a scroll it is valid.`,
  "siman_288/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 288 Taz note 2) Does not require processing for its sake. And see Yerushalmi chapter 3 of Yoma (halachah 6) and in Ketzot HaChoshen there dh "hide that was processed," and in Sefer VeShav HaKohen (siman 5).`,

  // batch 8 — simanim 207, 214, 217, 218, 222, 250, 253, 260, 263, 271, 274, 280, 284
  "siman_207/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|א": `Synonyms of vows, etc. — Rambam wrote in chapter 1 of Laws of Vows, law 16: there are places whose people are playful and corrupt the language and designate one thing by another — there one follows the designation; how so — any synonym of korban is like korban: one who says "these are upon me, konam, konach, konas" — these are synonyms of korban; and so all similar cases — one follows the language of the people of that place at that time — end. And so Semag in negative precepts, Rama, Ramban, and Ran at the beginning of Niddah in the name of the great sage R. Yitzchak bar R. Chisdai: we are taught that not only in gentile languages that are complete languages is one who vows in them bound by his vow, but even in languages that are not complete, such as these konam konach — which are holy tongue but corrupted — one might think one who vows in them is not bound since it is not a complete vow language in itself — it teaches that since gentiles speak them, even though it is not from their root language but from corruption in their hands, it is a complete vow; and all the more other gentile languages — end. And such are the words of Tur and the Rav; and see siman 237 in the gloss.`,
  "siman_207/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|1|_": `Konam konach, etc. — it is explicit in the mishnah and Gemara first chapter of Nedarim (10a) that konam too is a synonym, and all the synonyms are synonyms of korban; and that which he mentioned in the formula "all my vows and my konamim and my konasim and my synonyms" — he means konami is the most common synonym, as it is mentioned first in the mishnah for synonyms, and afterward the mishnah mentions konas — that is "and my konasim"; therefore he said afterward "and my synonyms," meaning all other synonyms; and as much as Tur and Shulchan Aruch wrote here "and in every place one follows," so it appears to me.`,
  "siman_214/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1|ו": `Rashba in a responsum and Ran in chapter 4 of Pesachim in the second explanation of Yerushalmi's words.`,
  "siman_214/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|2|_": `Rivash siman 70 in the name of Ramban in Laws of Ban.`,
  "siman_217/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|11|א": `In whey — Rashi, Rosh, and Ran explained this is the water separated from milk; and see siman 81 seif 5 and siman 87 seif 8.`,
  "siman_217/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|48|א": `That he not laugh, etc. — see Derishah siman 215 and Beit Chadash siman 216 on laws of laughter; and see siman 228 seif 15 and so forth.`,
  "siman_218/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2|א": `We follow his intention — these are Rashbatz's words that Beit Yosef brought; and although this law opens with great ones and closes with great ones, nevertheless it is Torah and I must learn, for not only was it difficult for him from Talmudic words but from his own words as we will explain: that which he wrote that we follow intention is astounding — for we hold like Shmuel that mouth and heart must agree, as written above siman 210; and what do we care about his intention since he explicitly said he will not make — implying what is already made is not included; and explicitly regarding "you shall not make for yourself a graven image" — I have only "you shall not make"; from where not to maintain what is already made? Scripture teaches "it shall not be for you" — behold what is already made is not included; and in Teshuvot HaRashba siman 277 he wrote we judge according to intention even if against his utterance — clearly meaning we do not interpret the language according to its plain meaning, as above regarding wool-wearing prohibition per Rosh; but nevertheless it can be interpreted according to intention, unlike when it completely contradicts as here; and what Rashbatz brought as proof from one who vows wool shall not go upon him in seif 1 that we follow intention — does not apply, for there the language is not contradicted at all, unlike here; and moreover he contradicts himself, for at the end of siman 208 Beit Yosef in his book brings Rashbatz: one who says he will not marry a woman within three years — if he said language implying he will not perform marriage with her and he transgressed and married, it is like one who swore on a loaf and ate it — he is asked and they permit him; but if he said language implying she will not be his wife within three years they do not permit — and they ruled in Shulchan Aruch there; behold before us that we follow the language's meaning and do not say "what he said — he will not perform marriage" means she will not be his wife; so too here one who said he will not make wine does not mean he will not have wine — it is one matter; and moreover it is a fortiori: there, where it is close that he did not insist on the marriage time — for what does he care — but only that he not have a collar hanging on his neck and be busy with his wife's needs, and this matter he included in performing marriage since marriage is the beginning and he took the beginning — nevertheless we say we follow the language as plain; all the more here where he did not intend to lose what he already has for fear of danger but only not to enter anew into making wine — i.e., preventing profit — therefore he said specifically "will not make"; all the more we follow the language and only what left his mouth is forbidden; and moreover if two kinds were dangerous to sell to a gentile and he swore on one — would we follow intention that he fears danger and forbid the second? This is certainly not so; here too are two kinds — preventing profit and saving from loss — as we find this distinction regarding lost objects in Choshen Mishpat and mourning siman 380 seif 6 that one is not like the other; here too likewise.`,
  "siman_218/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2|ב": `And all the more a community, etc. — the Rav learned this from Rashbatz's ruling that Shulchan Aruch already mentioned; and I say even if no place were given to that ruling as we recalled, nevertheless this law is true and explicit in Rosh's words first chapter of Chullin on the question the Gemara asks regarding nostrils of cattle that Israelites brought with them to Eretz Yisrael — what is the law; Rosh explained it emerges regarding one who forbids himself one species from a known time onward, and when the time arrived he had of that species he ate until the time — whether he may eat what remained; or if a court wanted to forbid something — whether what he has of that species in an Israelite's hand is forbidden — end; and since it remained in his pouch we are stringent biblically; yet we can say on the contrary from there is proof to permit here, since this remained in his pouch and every rabbinic doubt is lenient, and here too is rabbinic, for there is no prohibition except from the community's enactment; and so I saw in Teshuvot HaRashba siman 281 who brought in the name of one of the great ones who ruled regarding a community enactment not to sell meat above the price they set — and one had slaughtered meat to sell dear since he had it before the enactment — he brought proof from this question and wrote in the name of Baal Teshuvot HaRashba that since it was merely an enactment and not an oath; but whenever they made a ban it is biblical and we are stringent; and he wrote afterward: although I have some hesitation on that ruling of Semag who wrote one who swears not to enter the community enactment is considered a vain oath — apparently community enactment is also biblical — he concludes that in vows that are only rabbinic, such as on something without substance, etc., it is a rabbinic doubt for leniency; therefore according to this there is no place for the "all the more" Rama wrote here regarding community enactment — for first Shulchan Aruch wrote regarding one who swore and an oath is biblical — how does he derive from this regarding enactment that is not from Torah; and although Teshuvot HaRashba wrote he has some hesitation on enactment, nevertheless it is clear there his intent is not to dispute and be stringent on enactment and nullify the practical ruling of that great one; and indeed I already wrote in siman 228 seif 33 to resolve Teshuvot HaRashba's hesitation well — specifically where it is to fence a matter of sin it is biblical, unlike where it is to save money or other reasons not related to sin. I again saw in Rashal first chapter of Chullin siman 36 who brought this Rosh explanation on the question we mentioned — Rosh's first answer that it emerges regarding one who forbids himself, etc. — follows that it is a rabbinic doubt, for whenever a person forbids himself in vow language saying he will not eat a certain thing without attaching a forbidden matter it is only rabbinic; and he disputes Rosh on this, for in vows one follows people's language and according to people's language it is forbidden to eat from this species when the time arrives; therefore the second answer of Rosh applies regarding when a court wanted to forbid, etc. — rabbinic doubt for leniency; nevertheless specifically when a court wanted to forbid, but if they decreed with ban and curse not to eat this thing it is biblical, for a court has power to ban and excommunicate even beyond the law if their intent is to fence; if so it is a biblical doubt and stringent; and according to this what was done in days when sages of Russia gathered the majority of the people not to drink ordinary Israelite wine unless notable witnesses testify to its kashrut and they limited time for their decree — all that already remained in their hands is also forbidden, for it is a biblical doubt — end Rashal; behold in enactment without ban he clearly permits in doubt; and since in Rama's case it was rabbinic doubt we are lenient and there is no prohibition unless the enactment was with community ban; and even here there is a practical difference if that remaining thing became mixed in some mixture where there is doubt — it is a double doubt since the prohibition itself is not clear but only from what remained in his pouch in the Gemara; and when a doubtful prohibition became mixed — when is it double doubt — was explained in siman 110 above end of seif 9 that two mixtures are required; unlike if the source were Rashbatz's words and the prohibition were certain — it would not be called double doubt except with three mixtures as written in siman 110 there; and truth will teach his way that this is only doubtful prohibition as Rosh explicitly explained — so appears clear to me.`,
  "siman_222/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|_": `And even, etc., and some say, etc. — Erech Netaim there dh "and regarding," and in Even HaEzer siman 85 seif 11.`,
  "siman_222/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_": `Provided — Beit HaTov in the name of Shach. And see in Sefer Sha'ar HaMelekh end of chapter 7 of Laws of Vows who wondered on him that he omitted Ran's words first chapter of Kiddushin that it is explicit there that even if he did not say "on condition that your husband has no rights in them" it is effective; and so from the language of Rosh's abbreviated rulings there; and so explicit from Rashba's novellae; also Rav Tosafot Yom Tov in Nedarim there inferred thus from Rambam's words — see there; and from the mishnah that teaches both — one cannot infer to say the mishnah comes to teach only "on condition your husband has no rights" alone is ineffective until we say what you give; and so too regarding what you give alone it is effective — see there.`,
  "siman_250/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|3|_": `Tur in the name of Rambam chapter 7 of Laws of Positive Precepts from the incident of a certain poor man who went door to door who came before R. Papa [Bava Batra 9a].`,
  "siman_250/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|4|ב": `Shabbat 118a.`,
  "siman_253/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ב": `But from scraping — per Rif's explanation there. (Extract) Scraping — see Tosafot Bava Metzia 113a s.v. "and you," etc. (end).`,
  "siman_253/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|4|_": `For some disagree — [and there Rama ruled like the first view; and in responsum Maharival part 2 siman 67 he concludes that even if orphans' heirs seized we remove from him, for he cannot say "I hold like Ravyah"; and see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 238 what he wrote on this].`,
  "siman_260/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|_": `And greater, etc. — mishnah Nedarim 41b and Gemara there 32a; baraita: Rabbi says, etc.; another version: greater, etc.`,
  "siman_260/yad-avraham/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 260) And this mitzvah is greater than other positive commandments. Beit Yosef wrote it appears this is apart from the mitzvah of tzitzit, for Tur in Orach Chayim siman 24 wrote tzitzit is equivalent to all mitzvot — i.e., all 613; if so what is special here about milah that it is greater than other positive commandments — it must be apart from tzitzit which is greater; and he does not hold like those who say in Nedarim (32a) that milah is equivalent to all mitzvot — and this is incorrect, for how can tzitzit be greater than milah which has karet and overrides Shabbat, unlike tzitzit; and moreover in Sifrei parashat Shelach it says: you shall see them and remember — it teaches that whoever fulfills tzitzit is elevated as if he fulfilled all mitzvot; and it concludes a fortiori for all Torah mitzvot — if so on the contrary it implies tzitzit is lighter than other positive commandments; and so in Menachot (44a) — see Tzafnat Paneach; nevertheless fulfillment is considered as fulfilling all mitzvot, not that it is greater than them; and for this Tur in Orach Chayim intended; and we find this language in Bava Batra regarding charity that is equivalent, etc., and regarding Shabbat in Yerushalmi; and so regarding Torah study.`,
  "siman_263/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|5|ב": `Conversion — Taz wrote: it appears the reason is since we say Avraham our father stands and examines whoever is circumcised — he does not let him go to Gehenna; and this one who circumcised the gentile removes the sign of circumcision from Israel — end. But Shach explained he does so for healing, e.g., the gentile needs to cut his foreskin because of a wound and the like, and it is forbidden to heal him for free but for payment it is permitted — see there.`,
  "siman_263/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5|ד": `For even, etc. — Shabbat there.`,
  "siman_271/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|4|_": `And fowl — see in Sefer Derech Chaim on Laws of Shechitah and Tereifot siman 23 in the gloss who wrote that from Noda BiYehudah second series part 14 siman 12 it seems obvious to him that bird hide has the law of gevil, klaf, and duchsustos mentioned regarding books, tefillin, and mezuzot — doubtful, for we never heard such in Gemara and poskim on bird hide which is thin; and Rambam chapter 1 of Laws of Tefillin explicitly infers the law of klaf and duchsustos is specifically cattle and wild animal hide; bird hide without this distinction — one may say it has the law of our klafim; nevertheless it is doubtful on which side one writes — whether on the hair side as our klaf law or on every side — end.`,
  "siman_271/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|7|_": `That he say at the outset — Beit HaTov. And in Beit Yosef he brought responsum of Rama Azulai that post facto intent suffices [and this is siman 94; and so responsum Radbaz part 1 siman 154; also in Be'er HaGolah below siman 274; and in my compositions Even HaEzer siman 151 seif 7 I expanded somewhat on this].`,
  "siman_274/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|3|א": `Baraita Shabbat 103b.`,
  "siman_274/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|5|א": `Or similar, etc. — Shabbat there.`,
  "siman_280/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1|ב": `More — Nachalat Kehuna wrote he found in many responsa they ruled that even more than three stitches one may sew, and there is no doubt if the Rav had seen these poskim's words he would not have written so — end; and Bach wrote our sefer Torah according to all may be sewn, for the threads are white and our klafim too are white — therefore it is known stitches are not noticeable in a sefer Torah and even three may be sewn; and so to glue klaf on tears with glue.`,
  "siman_280/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 280 seif 1) But not more. And so they agreed for practical law in responsum Bach (siman 108), responsum Shevut Yaakov (part 1 siman 80), responsum Maharit (part 1 siman 31); and see responsum Rashbash (siman 150) who wrote they acted so in his community — a sefer Torah torn within four lines, they spread a cloth on it and permitted reading from it; and see responsum Perach Matmon (part 1 siman 45), responsum Emunat Shmuel (siman 56), and responsum Rav Alashkar (siman 90) who wrote Rivash later conceded that even more than three one may sew — and it appears thus is Chiddushei HaGershuni's view.`,
  "siman_284/nekudot-hakesef/part-001.txt|2|_": `(There in Shulchan Aruch seif 2) Three words, etc. — so ruled most poskim and later authorities; so Bach; and it appears to me to bring proof for their words from end of Laws of Chalitzah where they say there Abaye wrote "get of chalitzah" and read for himself from "lo" until "lekachtah" so as not to require ruling lines; and so Mar Zutra ruled lines for the whole section even "lo chafatzti lekachtah" — only three words; apparently Abaye and Mar Zutra hold two one writes, three one does not write [and Rosh who wrote that which is said "and read for himself from lo until lekachtah" — I do not know why, for we hold three one writes] — it appears Rosh's intent is not to challenge Abaye and Mar Zutra, for there is one who says in first chapter of Gittin that three one does not write; and moreover Rosh himself ruled so in first chapter of Megillah that three one does not write; rather Rosh's intent is to challenge Rashi's explanation there who explained their reason is because we hold three one writes, four one does not write — and if so they should have written "lo chafatzti lekachtah" without ruling lines (and so one can challenge Nimukei Yosef who also wrote like Rashi — and examine well).`,

  // batch 9 — final 22 MT blocks in 201-300
  "siman_209/nekudot-hakesef/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 209 Taz small seif 1) And his explanation is astounding, etc. — Lechem Mishneh: since he mentioned at the beginning that it is accepting his words, he mentioned at the end likewise; and so even against his will he can forbid; and according to his explanation it is difficult how he says "but when his fellow imposes a vow on him," etc., for he cannot impose a vow on him; and moreover from Torah he would need to accept his words, for here we deal with a vow and he is not accustomed to utter an oath; and moreover how is this law relevant here; and moreover what is "but" — the matter is simple as Beit Yosef explained.`,
  "siman_215/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|3|ב": `And even if he did not accept upon himself to fast except on Shabbat and festivals — meaning not only at the beginning where he did not intend at all for Shabbat and festivals, but even if he explicitly accepted to fast on Shabbat and festivals, nevertheless the vow does not take effect since he did not say it in language of "behold upon me"; and gratuitously Mahari Chai wrote on Shulchan Aruch's language here that it is not precise. Incident: a man vowed to give from the profit of his property a tithe and so practiced several days, then his hand slackened and he regretted — it appears he can retract because profit never came and one does not consecrate what never came, as explained in chapter "An ox that gored four and five"; however if he said "this money — all profit reaching from it I will give a tithe from it," consecration takes effect since consecration came into existence — this Derishah brought in the name of Rashal in the name of Me'il Tzedek; we do not hold so, rather as written in Choshen Mishpat siman 212 seif 9: one who says when I take it I will give it to consecration — a vow name is upon him and he must fulfill his vow — so it appears to me. He further brought a gloss in his name that R. Eliezer of Metz: one who vows to fast a month — they tell him in this month you vowed there are festivals and Shabbatot; he says I did not intend them — he needs no release, for hidden matters — these are evident matters — end; and it appears this is like Sefer HaMitzvot we mentioned; and according to what we hold like Ramban, here too release is required whenever he said in vow language as above, except if he said explicitly it was on his mind not to fast except weekdays — he is believed, for he does not contradict his vow language of fasting one month; thereby the above gloss and Shulchan Aruch seif 2 requiring release is not about this but when festivals and Shabbat did not occur to him at vow time. Another gloss in his name: a woman vowed to fast after second and fifth days of Passover until Rosh Chodesh; after Passover they asked when she begins — it appears after Nissan days if the custom everywhere is not to say supplications all Nissan days — end; and Mahari Chai wrote this is specifically in language of accepting a fast generally, but in vow language release is required since she explicitly vowed to fast after Passover — no better than one who vows to fast on a festival.`,
  "siman_223/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|_": `He forbade his son, etc. — Erech Netaim 49a s.v. "and regarding," etc.; but Rambam, etc.`,
  "siman_225/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1|א": `Tur's language and so Rambam chapter 5 of Laws of Vows from Rav Nachman and Rav Amram's statement, etc., 40b.`,
  "siman_226/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|1|ח": `And so if, etc. — see in Even HaEzer siman 258 seif 7 in the gloss.`,
  "siman_230/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|1|ג": `Slight sin, etc. — see siman 228 seif 15.`,
  "siman_231/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1|ב": `Time — meaning immediately at vow time he annuls it; or he conditioned the vow is not a vow until his fellow hears and they do not release — then the vow does not take effect until he hears and immediately upon hearing he annuls — then the vow never took effect at all — Shach. Taz wrote: one said to his wife "give me 600 so I will not marry another after my death, and I too will dwell for you not to marry another after your death" — his vow does not take effect even if he already fulfilled 46 at vow time; from now her vow too does not take effect, for she vowed only on condition he fulfills his vow and she has release — and for clarity he should give her a release — responsum Azulai, Rashal; and see what Rama wrote siman 232 seif 16 — end Taz.`,
  "siman_243/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|2|ג": `Therefore they are exempt — Ramban wrote in his novellae first chapter of Bava Batra (8a): that which the Gemara says commoners said "let us flee from here" and Rabbi let them flee — that is where the rabbi cannot compel them; but if he could compel them it is proper to compel them, for since the king's burden is on everyone they are all obligated to give and men's skulls and property are at stake — end; and so in Niddah there.`,
  "siman_245/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|6|ב": `Possible — and all a person's sustenance is fixed for him from Rosh Hashanah except Shabbat and festival expenses and sending children to Torah study — if he adds, they add for him — Gemara Beitzah.`,
  "siman_272/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|4|ב": `And he makes, etc. — see Tosafot there s.v. "verse one," etc.; and so Rosh and Shulchan Aruch.`,
  "siman_273/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|6|א": `He forgot, etc. — like Rashash in Ulla's chapter there (see there) likewise, although in what he said "and not only," etc., the halachah is not like him as written in chapter 4 of Chullin (75b). Siftei Cohen and Shulchan Aruch (Av Beit Din and Shach below siman 276 s.k. 7; and so Rabbeinu in Orach Chayim siman 32 s.k. 79 left it unresolved).`,
  "siman_276/yad-avraham/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 276 in Shulchan Aruch seif 9) Forbidden to erase, etc., such as the kaf of "your God," etc. — in responsum of Mahari Segal on a sefer Torah who wrote the scribe on the name Elohim Eloheikhem may extend the bent kaf and make it a closed mem, and afterward may erase the mem since no letter is needed and he is not erasing or scraping any letter; and even though in writing he causes the final mem not to attach to the name. And there is to one who asks from Hagahot Maimoniot brought in this siman regarding one who should have written Hashem and wrote Yehudah — see there; and so in Shabbat.`,
  "siman_277/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|1|_": `And he shall spread — in last chapter of Eruvin we say where spreading a garment is impossible, one turns the writing, for if not turned there is greater disgrace of holy writing; and Bach wrote from here one learns a person should not go out and leave a book open without a cloth on it — not specifically sefer Torah but likewise other books; Shach wrote: and known to masters of truth there is an angel called Shad who stands and silences one who leaves a book open and goes out, causing him to forget his learning — end.`,
  "siman_278/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|3|_": `Around the pillars — see in Degel Machaneh Ephraim who wondered on Rama that in Tosafot there it deals with sewing sheets to each other, but regarding pillars even if not sewn at all in the sheet and there is nothing to sew — it is valid — see there; and so Bnei Yonah challenged him and raised that if they brought out a sefer Torah without pillars at all, they bring another that has pillars if found there; if not found there they read from this; but if it has pillars only the pillars were not sewn with the sheets — and all the more if sewn with silk even though another exists sewn with sinew — they do not bring another because of blemish to the first; and since so, obviously a sefer Torah without pillars is forbidden to hold bare — unlike what I saw people practice that as long as there are no pillars they are not concerned to hold it — see there [and see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 276 also on this matter why scribes are accustomed to sew sheet pillars of sefer Torah in klaf pieces and not sinew; he brought Degel Machaneh Ephraim and Bnei Yonah and added proof pillars are not essential and therefore may be sewn even in silk (impure matter from worm as Rabbeinu wrote in Chayei parashat Terumah); and wrote nevertheless since we compare, we do not act initially to sew in silk or even flax, but to sew with straps made from klaf is fine, for there is Baal HaIttur's view that one may initially sew tefillin and sefer Torah in klaf; and Shulchan Aruch Orach Chayim siman 32 seif 3 relies on him in time of pressure where sinew is unavailable to sew in taldros — and it appears therefore scribes sew pillars in taldros to show the halachah that pillars do not invalidate sefer Torah; and similarly Tosafot chapter HaKorei Omed; and the principle — custom of Israel is Torah — end; see there].`,
  "siman_281/nekudot-hakesef/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 281 Taz s.k. 1) His words — in explaining Tur's words — are not plausible; the matter is simple that because of the end clause Tur mentioned "and one does not read in it for endings" and even though one does not read in it, they are obligated to buy it from him, etc.; also what he meant invalid that is not evident — such as not processed for its sake — and similar should be forbidden to read even individually — is not plausible and not comparable to doubtful tereifah, for there specifically with many it is forbidden because we fear mishap; and even according to what we practice to be stringent even in one — that is specifically tereifah where we fear mishap, for it is disgraceful to eat forbidden matter; and similarly Tosafot first chapter of Shabbat 12b regarding Rabbi Yishmael who read and leaned — or because there we fear not everyone knows it is tereifah; or lest it go by itself to another house that does not know it is tereifah; but here since a sefer Torah's way is to be in synagogue and this one reads individually and never in synagogue — the matter is evident it is invalid and there is no misleading; and one can write on the column and similar that it is invalid.`,
  "siman_284/turei-zahav/part-001.txt|2|_": `Forbidden to write three words from a verse — as should be. And that which is written "and if he ruled an upper line," etc. — in Tur it is written: that one does not write without ruling applies specifically to the upper line; but from here onward the upper line is like ruling, as we say regarding tefillin that if he ruled the upper line he no longer needs ruling, since ordinarily people know to train their hand to write on the upper without ruling — end. And it is written in Derishah: it is difficult — what is tefillin's relevance to sefer Torah; and moreover why therefore I say that Rabbenu Tam said the essence of his words is about tefillin as Tosafot and Beit Yosef bring; and what Rabbeinu wrote "since he ruled," etc. — he means Rabbenu Tam wrote so regarding tefillin and Rosh said "therefore I say," etc. — meaning as Rabbenu Tam said regarding tefillin that ruling is needed only upper line, so too here no ruling except upper line, as we say in tefillin and there it is proven from the difficulty Rabbenu Tam proved from it — end; and apart from his honor he was not precise in understanding these matters — not this way; rather the intent is clear it refers to a letter that no ruling is needed when writing a verse name except if in the first line; but if no verse in first line, one writes there without ruling; and when writing a verse in the next line, no ruling needed there either since the first was written without ruling permissibly — it becomes as if there were a verse in the first with ruling for this; therefore he wrote "as regarding tefillin," etc. — no ruling except upper; and on this he gave reason that ordinarily a person knows to train his hand — meaning when no verse in upper and he writes without ruling, nevertheless he knows to write straight; this appears simple to me except the commentator understood Rosh here refers to sefer Torah and learned from tefillin and entered to explain what is not given to write — and it seems that did not come from that tzaddik's mouth but a mistaken student wrote it.`,
  "siman_285/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1|ב": `Baraita Shabbat 32a.`,
  "siman_285/yad-avraham/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 285 seif 1) And whoever is careful with it — his days are lengthened; and if not careful — shortened. And Tur concludes from affirmative you hear negative. Beit Yosef challenged from Kiddushin (61a) dispute of Rabbi Meir and Rabbis on double condition: for Rabbi Meir it is written "if you do well, bear up; if not well, sin crouches," "if you walk in My statutes," "if you despise My statutes," "if you consent and listen, the good of the land you shall eat," "if you refuse" — but for Rabbis why the doublings; answered: lest one say if you do well reward and if not no reward and no judgment — implying for all we do not derive punishment from affirmative alone. And to resolve: we conclude in chapter 5 of Shevuot that Rabbi Meir lacks "from affirmative you hear negative" in prohibition involving money, but in standalone prohibition he has it; and see Tosafot what they wrote that all those in Kiddushin involve money — if so Beit Yosef's question on mezuzah is no question at all, for there is no money; and similarly all say from affirmative we hear negative even for punishment; and accordingly one can resolve Maharsha's question in part 1 Shabbat why he did not challenge according to Rabbi; and similarly Ran did not give reason "that you multiply" teaches death regarding mezuzah and Torah study; and according to what was said one can say they hold like the initial suggestion that even regarding prohibition punishment is not derived from affirmative for one who requires doubling; and Rabbi holds doubling in Yevamot (53a).`,
  "siman_287/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|1|_": `Rava's statement Menachot 33a per Rashi's explanation.`,
  "siman_289/nekudot-hakesef/part-001.txt|1|_": `(Siman 289 Shulchan Aruch seif 2) At the beginning of the upper third, etc. — this is Rambam's view; accordingly some craftsmen in our generation place at beginning of upper third; but the whole world practices to place at door height per Rosh; and in my humble opinion they act well, for it appears primary in Gemara Menachot (53a); and those craftsmen should examine well — if they had examined Gemara they would not act so; and so explicit in Semag positive precepts 23 folio 106 end col. 3 — see there; and so Rabbeinu Yerucham Netiv 21 ch. 7 — see there. Again it appears clear Rambam's Gemara text did not read "not to distance from beam more than a third"; and so explicit in Tosafot and Rashi there — we do not have that text but we have: and what is "beginning of upper third" Shmuel said — for distance and nothing else (again I found explicitly this text in Gemara on klaf written hundreds of years ago); and Rambam explains not like Rashi and Tosafot but for distance — meaning Rabbi Yosei agrees with Rabbi Yehudah that distance from beam a handbreadth is required, and because of distance concern Shmuel said its mitzvah is to place at beginning of third which is distance and fence — that one place above a handbreadth; and now also resolves what Beit Yosef and Bach challenged — from where Rambam that distance above a handbreadth is required; and now also resolves Rambam did not explicitly write if fixed below this it is invalid — for if he explained like Rashi and Tosafot that Shmuel comes to exclude below, then Shmuel's main law Rambam did not write in explanation but in implication; rather certainly as I separated — clear Rambam's view, unlike Beit Yosef and Bach who forced his view; if so explicit from Tosafot and Rashi they hold like Rosh; and so Semag and Rabbeinu Yerucham as written; and so Rabbeinu Eliezer of Metz in Sefer Yereim siman 18: we say in HaKometz Rav Chisda said mitzvah to place at beginning of upper third — he explained not to lower more but to raise more is permitted; reason as Rabbi Yosei taught "you shall bind" and "you shall write" — as binding at hand and head height, so writing at door height; and so in Shiltot Gibborim wrote in Laws of Mezuzah: its mitzvah at beginning of upper third and above; therefore he says its mitzvah at upper third height; and in our opinion from upper third upward its mitzvah wherever one wishes as explained in Rashi's commentary; and such is commentators' method — end his language; if so the whole world who practice like most poskim act well.`,
  "siman_290/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt|1|_": `Sefer Torah or tefillin that wore out — [see responsum Chatam Sofer siman 279: a rabbi wanted to distinguish that specifically sefer Torah that wore out — holiness never departed; unlike missing and extra at start of writing — sanctity of sefer Torah never rested; as Rama wrote above siman 282 that one may sell it and therefore may also make mezuzah from it; and similarly cut its pages to repair a complete sefer Torah; and he wrote this is not comparable — that "wore out" is inclusive, meaning even worn out and no longer fit for its mitzvah at all, even to teach children — all the more when only missing and extra and can be repaired to read publicly or study — obviously one does not make mezuzah from it (and what he wrote there necessity for this — if you think it was never sanctified initially, then no sefer Torah was sanctified until the last of all Israel wrote — how sanctified retroactively; per what Noda BiYehudah first series siman 71 wrote regarding sefer Torah later invalidated — see there; now per what above siman 276 s.k. 3 in name of Rama Azulai and Bnei Yonah that its sanctification and completion come together — see there — this necessity is not required — examine well); and what Rivash wrote sefer Torah with error is only like a chumash — there regarding selling it, etc. — Eshel Avraham].`,
  "siman_299/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ב": `And some say, etc. — so Rash and Rosh there and in Niddah; and see siman 305 and what is written.`,

  // stragglers — siman 177 commentary MT blocks
  "siman_177/baer-heitev/part-001.txt|2|ד": `On accidents — and some say as above siman 173 seif 13 that one may accept vinegar liability; and Shach wrote it implies that in issur too one must accept all liability that applies — dearness and cheapness — and all the more when giving money for half profit, for even the lenient agree; and Hagahot Maimoniot wrote: even though one gives issur to his fellow on condition the agent takes three quarters of profit and the money owner a quarter, nevertheless it is half loan and half deposit, and what the agent takes more in profit is wage for toil — end.`,
  "siman_177/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt|25|_": `Rivash in responsum siman 335.`,
  "siman_177/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|2|ט": `And one must, etc., and not like, etc. — see Tosafot 7a s.v. "for he," etc.; and Shach.`,
  "siman_177/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|14|א": `One who says, etc., forbidden because, etc. — meaning even though by law it is permitted as written in Bava Batra 168a in the mishnah "one who paid," etc., and some explain only regarding asmakhta as written, but for interest there is no waiting fee since it is only for one moment that if that moment passes he will give, etc., and it is a penalty — nevertheless it is interest evasion as above; and in the mishnah there one can say he gives him the first money he paid as a gift. Rashba and Rivash siman 335. (Extract) One who says, etc. — Rashba siman 461 and so R. Yitzchak Migash in Bava Batra 168a like the mishnah there "one who paid," etc. (end). (Extract) One who says, etc. — as written "one who paid part of his debt," etc.; and in Bava Metzia 65b "he lent him on his field," etc., and the Gemara says he acquired everything even more in proportion to his money; and even one who disagrees — only because of asmakhta. Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba 2: but afterward he wavers to forbid initially because of interest evasion like interest evasion there 2b and hired workers, deferred interest, etc.; and the mishnah "he lent him," etc. — sale is different for it is like a sale; and mishnah "one who paid" — first payment retroactively a gift; and even for one who says any amount of zuz we remove him — here he admits it is worse than dust of interest and like deferred interest and lighter than it, except forbidden initially because of interest evasion — and this is what is written in siman 161 seif 2 "apart from," etc. (end). (Extract) One who says, etc. — but by law it is not interest since he is not obligated at all until that time and for one moment he became obligated even though in his mind it is because he profits with his money over time; proof from what is written in Ketubot 66a "she assessed to bring in," etc., and Rashi there s.v. "he assessed," etc., according to intent; and Mordechai wrote there it is not interest since if he divorces immediately he owes her; and so in Hagahot Ashiri s.v. "he explained," etc. (end). (Extract) One who says, etc. — as written above they wrote it is permitted by law since it is only one moment, except to be stringent lest they evade thus — and it is not clear to me, for we say in Bava Metzia 14b that for Shmuel it is interest and they did not say "it appears like interest" except because it is a sale as written 15; and when permitted, etc., there "a loan," etc.`,
  "siman_177/beur-hagra/part-001.txt|23|ד": `(Extract) And from then on, etc., and if he comes, etc. — what is written "and if he comes," etc., explains what is written above "and from then on," etc. — specifically when he divided before three, etc.; Taz and so Beit Yosef and so Rambam and Rashba in name of Rashi; but Tosafot disagree and write even division without appraisal stipulation he takes half-and-half, etc.; and so in baraita that teaches anonymously "from here onward," etc.; and so in Yerushalmi; and what Abaye said "who disagrees with you," etc. — two matters he said: first to nullify division, second to exempt entirely. And Shulchan Aruch follows Rashi and Rambam, unlike Rosh and Bach in last commentary who forced.`,
  "siman_177/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|3|_": `(Shach s.k. 49) And we extract from him against his will — end. And see responsum Bach (163).`,
  "siman_177/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt|4|_": `(Shach s.k. 68) Since it is a legal doubt. And see responsum Mahari of Lemberg (klal 3 sn 53) at length. And see responsum Teshuvot HaChacham (part 3 siman 17).`,
  "siman_177/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|6|ג": `In all rabbinic interest, etc. — it is difficult, for on the contrary in Teshuvot HaRashba there it is explicit that even biblical interest is permitted when one accepts all liability — he brings initially the margin in Tosafot who wrote in name of Rabbenu Tam and also HaAgur and Mordechai and responsum of Maharam on one who fixed with a youth to give him expenses, etc., that they hold one may lend even with fixed interest when the giver accepts all liability of the money whether accidents, theft, or loss; and afterward wrote there is a remedy that the lender be secure on principal — meaning the lender accepts he is not believed even by witnesses that they did not damage by his hand unless through the rabbi and shochet; and similarly we find poskim wrote in issur one may stipulate with him not to hide silver and gold pledges in earth and if he changes liability is on recipient even though all know the giver's intent is not that conditions be fulfilled, etc.; and likewise here — however no proof from this except to permit rabbinic interest such as half-profit issur, for even if recipient accepted explicitly without any other conditions it would only be rabbinic prohibition since he does not cut a fixed amount but if there is profit it falls in middle; but where he cuts explicitly with him and if conditions were not between them it would be biblical interest — who says we permit seeking sides and conditions to permit what Torah forbade, etc. — end; behold he forbade biblical interest only when borrower promises on principal per condition that witnesses are not believed except rabbi and shochet; but when lender accepts all liability without condition — then borrower is believed by oath or witnesses — even biblical interest is permitted; and so in Darkei Moshe: and it implies specifically biblical prohibition he was stringent; but rabbinic interest permitted to act; and further implies specifically in such case forbidden in biblical interest; but if in a matter where lender could lose principal such as all witnesses or borrower believed — even biblical interest permitted — end; except afterward wrote however in responsum Rivash siman 308...`,
  "siman_177/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|25|א": `A great man, etc. — Beit Yosef and Beit HaTov wrote: regarding another person, if he preceded and showed the issur and seized — we do not remove from him, for it is a mistaken condition; but regarding a great man, since it is obvious they did not stipulate forbidden matter — we remove from him; and Beit Yosef brings Darkei Moshe.`,
  "siman_177/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|31|א": `He takes them, etc. — and Mordechai wrote even minor orphans too he takes; and I wonder how one can take from minor orphans — for we do not accept testimony on them as in end of first chapter (and Choshen Mishpat siman 110) — end Beit Yosef. And I wonder at his words, for it is possible when testimony was accepted in their father's lifetime — in such case we collect from them as in Choshen Mishpat siman 108 — end Darkei Moshe; and so Bach wrote see there.`,
  "siman_177/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt|32|ב": `Obligated to pay — the first giver; and if he has nothing the recipient must pay the recipient-of-gift from him as a gift — not the buyer or recipient of gift with permission who bought from him or received gift from him, for that buyer or gift-recipient with permission bought from him not knowing it was from the issur. Perishah. And Bach in last commentary wrote: meaning even though the gift is not in the recipient-of-gift's hand but he changed and exchanged for another thing or sold or gave to others or lost — the recipient-of-gift is obligated from the recipient to pay the giver even though he did not know he gave from issur, for it is like a thief who paid a debt or credit that must be returned free (as explained Choshen Mishpat siman 356 seif 6); and all the more a thief who gave as gift; and it appears the same when the recipient gave to others cheaply to hide the issur from the giver — his law is like a thief on whom market regulation was made if buyer did not know it was from issur; if he knew his law is like buying from a known thief as above; and from Perishah's words it appears he did not understand thus and I do not know his reason — end; and in my humble opinion Perishah also holds thus, and what he wrote "that buyer or gift-recipient with permission bought," etc. — refers to the second case — examine.`,
};
